window.harnessChunks["rows-7b52e6b47a8d40b8.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-02T00:24:10Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/stefan1294/agent-orchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Orchestrator (tracks)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-02\">2026-10-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/stefan1294/agent-orchestrator/tree/f78e1b06d80ed43803029a14f14df69908782fee\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f78e1b06d80ed43803029a14f14df69908782fee\">f78e1b0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement one bounded software feature through open-ended model-driven code/tool choices in its assigned worktree. - Disturbance / variety regulated: feature-specific repository structure, implementation choices, compiler/test feedback, debugging and local code interactions that deterministic orchestration rules cannot preselect. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose and revise semantic implementation actions in response to repository/tool feedback. - Decision owner: the model-backed Claude/Codex/Gemini implementation session launched by AgentExecutor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: feature prompt/acceptance steps, per-track worktree/branch, session persistence, max-turn/tool configuration, stop callback, Git status/commit checks, result capture. - Closure path: track dequeues feature → worktree/branch prepared → AgentExecutor launches model-backed implementation session → actor changes/tests code → session result and Git branch state return → orchestrator advances to verify, retry/failure or stop. - Boundary reachability: the built-in AgentExecutor constructs and launches supported coding-agent CLIs in the standard runtime; no application-level adapter implementation is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed actor leaves queue/Git/session enforcement but no component that can choose the feature&#39;s open-ended implementation. - Evidence: README.md; src/server/services/orchestrator.ts; src/server/services/agent-executor.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external agent internals remain outside the assessment boundary; only the first-party-reachable operational cell is credited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concrete interference among parallel implementation S1 cells over repository/worktree, category/track capacity, shared Git integration and verification. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous workers can collide in one checkout/base branch, multiple categories compete for track capacity, a merge can invalidate another track&#39;s branch, and simultaneous merge/verification operations can corrupt a shared live validation state. - Distinct S1 units: implementation sessions running on separate configured tracks/features. - Inter-S1 disturbance: shared repository/Git metadata and base-branch mutation, cross-track merge ordering, category-to-track contention and concurrent live verification can make otherwise valid worker actions interfere. - Attenuating coordination relation: one worktree per track, category routing, one active feature per track loop, Git mutex, verification mutex, feature-branch update before merge, serialized merge+verification, priority queues and deterministic track allocation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: routing determines which track executes a feature; retry/resume priority changes later dequeue order; a merge/update conflict or verification lock delays/stops later work; new base-branch state is merged into feature branches before their integration. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited mechanisms explicitly suppress evidenced cross-cell collisions in shared Git/base/live-verification resources, not merely move messages or enumerate tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: enforce track/worktree allocation and serialization relations that decide when and where an S1 may safely execute or enter shared integration/verification. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party queue/Git/orchestration machinery. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: QueueManager, GitManager worktrees/mutex, verification mutex, track definitions, branch-update/merge paths, session state. - Closure path: feature population is routed to tracks → isolated S1s execute → shared integration/verification is serialized and updated against latest base → resulting task/base state governs subsequent track execution. - Boundary reachability: all credited controls are integrated into normal start/track/merge/verify runtime paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removal of all model actors leaves materially the same allocation/isolation/serialization rules; the function is constructor-owned. - Evidence: src/server/services/queue-manager.ts; src/server/services/git-manager.ts; src/server/services/orchestrator.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: plurality/tracks alone are not the evidence; the state is grounded in concrete cross-cell Git/integration/verification attenuation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: regulate current fleet commitments/resources across tracks in response to active work, resume/retry priority, provider/rate-limit state, critical failures and shared merge/verification availability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: which current features/tracks should consume execution next, rate-limit/provider unavailability, repeated environment failures, a user-requested priority resume, merge/verification ownership and whether the factory should admit more work. - Whole-system current view: Orchestrator tracks global state, all configured track statuses/current sessions/queued counts, feature/session outcomes, a global resume request, critical-failure counters and serialized merge/verification ownership; dashboard combines full features, status and track definitions. - Current-control decision scope: base constructor routes/dequeues by track, prioritizes resume/retry, requeues rate-limited work, pauses other tracks for a resume request, auto-pauses a repeatedly failing track, serializes merge/verify and can stop the factory on unsafe Git/commit/merge failure. Parent mode can configure track allocation, start/stop, retry a feature with context, or prioritize/resume one feature over other tracks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: base mode — deterministic Orchestrator changes current allocation/admission/recovery under current fleet state; parent mode — local operator chooses current track/work priority and continuation interventions. - Decision owner: base C — deterministic Orchestrator/QueueManager state machine; parent P — legitimate local self-hosted human operator through dashboard/API. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: status projection, track/feature/session state, queue priorities, resume request, failure classification, critical counters, rate-limit delay, stop state, dashboard/API controls. - Closure path: current fleet state/exception is observed → deterministic rule or operator intervention selects an allowed current-control transition → queues/track/global state are mutated → later dequeue/execution follows the returned state. - Boundary reachability: both base runtime control and dashboard/API parent controls are shipped first-party surfaces over the same live Orchestrator state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-control choices in the base mode are rule-based; no model actor owns the whole-fleet managerial judgment. Parent mode is explicitly human-controlled. Model workers remain S1/S3 actors rather than S3 owners. - Evidence: src/server/services/orchestrator.ts; src/server/services/queue-manager.ts; src/client/pages/Dashboard.tsx; src/server/api/control.ts; src/server/api/retry.ts; src/server/api/resume.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic scheduling is not enough; the positive constructor state is grounded in whole-fleet priority, recovery, pause/stop and shared merge/verification transitions that alter present commitments.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the claim that an implemented/merged feature satisfies its acceptance steps using a separate verification session and observation-only role, then return failures into corrective execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementation agent can exit successfully or commit code that is behaviorally wrong, incomplete, or fails acceptance criteria after integration into the live base branch. - Claim being audited: the implemented feature works against the declared acceptance steps after its code is merged into the current base/live application. - Ordinary reporting path: implementation session exit/result, branch commits and agent output returned from the feature worktree. - Complementary access path: a newly created verification session runs from the main project root after merge/push and hot-reload; it receives a verification-only prompt, observes CLI/live-app behavior (and optionally browser behavior), and emits structured per-step PASS/FAIL findings. Supported Claude verification removes Edit from allowed tools. - Independence boundary: verification uses a separate session/context, different role/prompt, different working boundary (merged main project/live app rather than feature worktree), and supported read-only/restricted tools. It does not rely solely on the implementation session&#39;s self-report, even when the same configured model/agent family is reused. - Who acts on findings: first-party Orchestrator consumes the verdict; failure output is passed to a separate fix session, fix changes are committed and remerged, and a fresh verifier session challenges the result again until pass or bounded exhaustion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false-positive implementation completion and regressions visible only after integration/live observation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge acceptance-step PASS/FAIL from complementary runtime evidence; that judgment determines whether the feature is accepted or returned into the fix loop. - Decision owner: the model-backed verification actor in the first-party verification session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verification-only prompt, separate session record, main-root/live observation, restricted Claude tools, optional browser MCP, regex safeguard for explicit FAIL verdicts, bounded verify/fix attempts, verification mutex. - Closure path: implementation/merge produces ordinary success claim → separate verifier probes declared acceptance steps through complementary live/main-root access → PASS accepts feature; FAIL output is fed to fix agent → changes are remerged → new verification session retests → terminal pass/fail updates feature state. - Boundary reachability: the verification/fix loop is directly integrated into normal verifyAndMerge; no application-provided verifier implementation is required. Browser access is optional; CLI verification alone supplies the positive path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code controls session creation and consumes verdicts, but it cannot semantically test arbitrary acceptance steps itself. Removing the verification model actor eliminates the complementary behavioral judgment. - Evidence: README.md; src/server/services/orchestrator.ts; src/server/services/agent-executor.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the verifier can reuse the same underlying agent family as implementation, so independence is role/session/access-path rather than provider diversity. The positive finding relies on that structural separation plus live/main-root observation and corrective return; verification-disabled mode does not carry S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then intelligence loop is established that senses external/prospective change, generates adaptation options and returns them into current organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current framework detection, current provider/rate-limit failures, feature definitions and session history are observed, but these are operational/current-task distinctions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for prospective adaptation. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: framework detection, settings, session history, failure classification, fallback agents, browser capability and persistent progress. - Closure path: no external/future model → adaptation option → current-capability return loop found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fallback/retry reacts to present execution failures and framework detection configures current operation. Persistence/history does not itself synthesize strategic adaptation. - Evidence: README.md; src/server/services/orchestrator.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a future learning/planning capability could change this result; current automation is reactive.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party organizational identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project configuration, track/category allocation, base branch, prompts, agent/model selection, browser/tool settings and operator start/stop authority constrain operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity/ultimate-policy issue is adjudicated and returned as governing organizational policy. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: config/settings, ORCHESTRATOR instructions, dashboard controls, agent allowlists/tools, branch/verification policy. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue → ultimate authority → authoritative decision → return-to-operation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: operator configuration and S3 current-control intervention are bounded operational authority, not constitutional identity closure; prompts/instruction files are supplied rules, not S5 decisions. - Evidence: README.md; src/client/pages/Tracks.tsx; src/server/api/control.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a self-hosted user may externally define project policy, but the frozen product does not operationalize that broader authority as an S5 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/18cd57e3543a68cdf42c52ada3584a19d04a5179/assessments/agent-orchestrator-stefan1294.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 18cd57e3543a68cdf42c52ada3584a19d04a5179\">18cd57e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T22:20:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/escapeboy/agent-fleet-o\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FleetQ</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/escapeboy/agent-fleet-o/tree/d949ff4510548171b8a8648c794a7efd9d9d8c8c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d949ff4510548171b8a8648c794a7efd9d9d8c8c\">d949ff4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: complete bounded assigned crew tasks via model-driven and tool-bearing operational work units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: goal ambiguity, tool outputs, task-specific information/operation demands and errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose contextual model responses and tools/steps to satisfy assigned tasks within agent/crew constraints. - Decision owner: model-backed agent instantiated through first-party FleetQ ExecuteAgentAction within a registered crew, not unbounded vendor-specific orchestration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: job queue, crew roster and tool allowances, provider gateway, result persistence, budget/middleware, retry. - Closure path: worker task and approved tool context → model-led operation and environment replies → first-party result/outcome persistence → crew workflow. - Boundary reachability: ExecuteCrewTaskJob concretely calls first-party ExecuteAgentAction as a shipped job; live provider credentials and queue workers required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: worker model actually selects work behavior, while task/queue/config merely structures it. - Evidence: app/Domain/Crew/Jobs/ExecuteCrewTaskJob.php; app/Domain/Agent/Actions/ExecuteAgentAction.php. - Basis: installed source and configured model operations. - Confidence: medium-high in implementation boundary, not provider task success. - Caveats: no external model behavior is claimed as observed performance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate double-claim competition for one pending crew task among independent self-claim workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two concurrent agent cells might both claim/run the same pending task and duplicate or corrupt attribution and work effects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: atomically reserve the task for one agent and expose its changed assigned state to subsequent work selection. - Decision owner: first-party transactional claim constructor enforcing the self-claim mode&#39;s work custody rule; not a discretionary peer negotiator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DB::transaction, lockForUpdate, status filter Pending, Assigned status update and attributed agentid. - Closure path: concurrent claimant workers → one database row lock / pending predicate → one assigned worker, other no longer finds pending row → later work/claims follow updated occupancy. - Boundary reachability: first-party ClaimNextTaskAction in configured self-claim crew mode; ordinary serial/hierarchical modes need not execute it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic arbitration protects concurrent sibling operations against a specific duplicate-work disturbance; generic queueing alone would not. - Distinct S1 units: independent members of the self-claim crew independently asking for pending work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: both might execute the same task because of concurrent reads of unclaimed work. - Attenuating coordination relation: pessimistic locked database transaction selecting and assigning one pending row. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: losing claimant&#39;s later selection sees changed status and has to claim other work or idle. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: enforced exclusive work reservation protects competing S1 decisions, directly changing which worker may act. - Evidence: app/Domain/Crew/Actions/ClaimNextTaskAction.php; app/Domain/Crew/Services/CrewOrchestrator.php. - Basis: source-visible atomic claim. - Confidence: high for bounded structural prevention of double claims. - Caveats: does not ensure agents never create conflicting artifacts across different tasks.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: select and regulate live crew-wide work commitments and task ownership. - Disturbance / variety regulated: completed/failed/pending tasks, available specialists, bounded budget and need for replanning. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model coordinator chooses delegate/complete, assigned worker, next bounded task and, for experiment-linked crews, spawning subordinate experiment capacity. - Decision owner: configured model-backed coordinator agent, whose response is parsed and applied by first-party CoordinatorDecisionJob; middleware is enforcement, not owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stored CrewExecution, task status/outputs, AiGatewayInterface, createAndDispatchTask, first-party orchestrator and worker jobs. - Closure path: current crew completion/pending task view → model chooses next portfolio action → job creates/dispatches task or closes goal → changed task state and future coordinator iterations use updated current frontier. - Boundary reachability: hierarchical crew mode&#39;s CrewOrchestrator dispatches coordinator jobs; no claim that every crew process type uses the autonomous coordinator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model coordinator leaves only static process dispatch, not its context-dependent choice of whole-crew commitments. - Whole-system current view: current crew goal, configured workers, pending and completed/QA-failed task inventory, associated outputs and iteration count. - Current-control decision scope: selecting work assignments, new delegated task generation and closure at the crew recursion, not tenant-global authority. - Evidence: app/Domain/Crew/Jobs/CoordinatorDecisionJob.php; app/Domain/Crew/Services/CrewOrchestrator.php. - Basis: executable model-backed whole-current selection. - Confidence: medium-high within hierarchical mode. - Caveats: no observed live coordinator decision or evidence of full company-level management autonomy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: construct a separate, dataset-grounded replay/score challenge to candidate agent capability/configuration claims and return that evidence as a gate on subsequent changes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model/prompt regression that would be missed by current worker self-reports or success-status summaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: compare candidate agent responses against distinct eval cases and configured criteria, calculate threshold score and hold the candidate config update when below threshold. - Decision owner: first-party evaluation replay and configured deterministic gate (C), with a separate LLM judge supplying scored evaluation in the constructed path; no independent evidence of company-wide auditor S3=A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ReplayEvaluationDatasetAction, LlmJudge, separate cases/expected outputs, EvaluateAgentConfigGateAction, MCP agent update refusal. - Closure path: proposed agent config → replay curated dataset separately against candidate → independent evaluator and threshold → nonpassing verdict → AgentUpdateTool returns held and does not apply candidate, altering future agent operations. - Boundary reachability: opt-in agent.evalgate.enabled + configured dataset ID, wired into shipped MCP agentupdate path. It is not an unconditional gate on every role/config API. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: constructor captures independently curated examples and enforces them beyond routine task output QA; structured LLM judge output is a component, not proof of independently managed first-party auditor ownership. - Claim being audited: candidate prompt/model/agent configuration is safe enough to replace current operational capability. - Ordinary reporting path: worker task status/output, QA self-report feedback and aggregate crew summary. - Complementary access path: separately curated expected eval cases, re-execution against proposed config and independent scoring. - Independence boundary: dataset cases are not the producer&#39;s own completion message, and are rerun on the candidate, though judge/model independence and dataset coverage are not guaranteed. - Who acts on findings: candidate update MCP tool holds a below-threshold configuration change before it mutates persisted agent settings; operator can investigate/modify. - Evidence: app/Domain/Evaluation/Actions/ReplayEvaluationDatasetAction.php; app/Domain/Agent/Actions/EvaluateAgentConfigGateAction.php; app/Mcp/Tools/Agent/AgentUpdateTool.php; app/Domain/Evaluation/Services/LlmJudge.php; app/Domain/Crew/Actions/ValidateTaskOutputAction.php. - Basis: configured executed first-party comparator/return constructor. - Confidence: medium-high only in enabled gate with available dataset. - Caveats: infra failure is fail-open, and current ordinary QA consumes producer-provided output; neither implies universal successful independent verification of tasks.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: parent-governed future improvement of an agent&#39;s working role/personality based on execution history and optional actual user feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurring task failures, feedback and future behavior/capability mismatch after environmental use. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human operator authorizes/refuses a proposed change to future agent config; model analyzes external performance evidence and proposes options, but cannot cause the effectful promotion without approval. - Decision owner: human maintainer/operator using the shipped EvolutionProposal approval/apply UI or equivalent MCP/API process. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model-proposed EvolutionProposal, recent execution logs/negative feedback, Livewire panel approve/apply, ApplyEvolutionProposalAction approved-status gate. - Closure path: past runs/negative user feedback → model suggests changed goals/backstory/personality intended to improve later capability → legitimate user approves and applies → persisted managed agent context changes for subsequent executions. - Boundary reachability: EvolutionProposalPanel.analyze/approve/apply and ApplyEvolutionProposalAction are shipped; this is an explicit user-driven optional mode, not automatic evolution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent proposes, but model suggestions are insufficient to enact future organizational adaptation. Human selection is the decisive authority, hence P, not A or A(P). - External distinction: failures and feedback from actual crew executions, including user correction/negative ratings, are not merely self-authored future tasks. - Future / prospective distinction: proposes modifications to reusable agent goal/backstory/personality for later runs, not merely completing the current prompt. - Adaptation option generated: model-generated structured proposedchanges saved with rationale and confidence for an agent. - Path back into current capability / S3: explicit human approved/applied update persists new agent configuration subsequently used by the crew. - Evidence: app/Domain/Agent/Actions/AnalyzeAgentFeedbackAction.php; app/Domain/Evolution/Actions/AnalyzeExecutionForEvolutionAction.php; app/Livewire/Evolution/EvolutionProposalPanel.php; app/Domain/Evolution/Actions/ApplyEvolutionProposalAction.php. - Basis: shipped model-assisted, parent-owned option selection and persisted effect. - Confidence: medium in parent-mode closure, not improvement efficacy. - Caveats: the negative-feedback analyzer may require at least three qualifying ratings, and new proposals require deliberate parent authorization.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct legitimate identity/ethos/ultimate-policy decision path with subsequent whole-crew normative closure is established by the inspected first-party controls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: fundamental team identity/mandate, ethical principles and unresolved tensions between present crew delivery and prospective adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an ultimate-policy/identity question brought to legitimate authority and returned as an organization-level governing decision — absent as a distinct function-specific path in reviewed modes. - Decision owner: humans configure the team, agent/tool policies and permission approvals, but the evidence does not show this as an actual recurring ultimate-policy settlement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent policies, tool-risk approval, tenancy and role access, constraints, kill switch and versioned execution policy. - Closure path: existing policy is executed on actions and can be edited by users, yet no policy-identity issue → ultimate authority choice → governing return channel is identified beyond operational safety controls. - Boundary reachability: shipped agent/approval policy and crew identity configuration inspected; this is not a claim that a deploying organization lacks S5 outside FleetQ. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: names, permissions, tenant roles and risk approvals do not autonomously regulate ultimate identity. P is not warranted by ordinary approval alone. - Evidence: app/Domain/Approval/Services/AgentPolicyResolver.php; app/Domain/Approval/Services/PolicyEvaluator.php; app/Domain/Agent/Models/AgentPolicyVersion.php; app/Domain/Crew/Models/Crew.php. - Basis: bounded negative after checking plausible shipped policy/identity surfaces. - Confidence: medium-high for reviewed recursion and modes. - Caveats: wider tenant/company policy authority is outside the declared crew.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e80322036011a110bac5a74b73e4d4ce4a46a384/assessments/fleetq.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e80322036011a110bac5a74b73e4d4ce4a46a384\">e803220 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T15:34:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nearai/ironclaw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IronClaw</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nearai/ironclaw/tree/b0b999d96781516ee05e6ba961d6f3ead900da96\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b0b999d96781516ee05e6ba961d6f3ead900da96\">b0b999d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute user-directed or background work through a model-driven plan/tool/observation loop and produce operational outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous requests, tool/service observations, model results, persistent context, capability availability and execution failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive next actions/tools and revise the plan from returned observations within configured authority. - Decision owner: the running IronClaw model agent/job. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: canonical executor/planner, durable turn state, capability resolver, authorization/approval gates, tool runtimes, checkpoints and model transport. - Closure path: request/job state → model decision → authorized capability/tool action → returned observation/checkpoint → subsequent model decision → result. - Boundary reachability: the shipped ironclaw composition invokes the first-party canonical agent-loop executor; no downstream harness must be supplied to create the operational model/tool loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime code transports and constrains actions, while the model loop owns the substantive choice of action and follow-up from current evidence. - Evidence: AGENTS.md, crates/loop/ironclawagentloop/README.md, README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider inference is external execution substrate, not an imported organizational owner.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate shared provider/system-capacity contention among distinct concurrently runnable job S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: too many jobs simultaneously consuming finite LLM-provider concurrency and local runtime resources. - Decisive decision or feedback right: admit a Pending job to execution only when a concurrency slot is available. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic scheduler/concurrency policy; no autonomous S2 actor is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated job contexts, MAXPARALLELJOBS, Pending queue and creation-order dispatch. - Closure path: concurrent runnable jobs exceed the configured shared-capacity envelope → excess work remains Pending → completion/cancellation frees a slot → scheduler admits queued work → subsequent S1 execution proceeds inside the bounded envelope. - Boundary reachability: parallel jobs and the bounded scheduler are standard first-party job behavior documented for the shipped instance; no downstream coordinator is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the S2-specific attenuation relation is first-party and operational, but the coordination decision is deterministic policy rather than a model agent choosing how to reconcile the competing units. - Evidence: docs/capabilities/jobs/jobs.mdx, docs/capabilities/routines/cron.mdx, .env.example. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: generic job sequencing is not the basis; the credited witness is bounded shared-capacity contention across concurrently runnable S1 jobs. - Distinct S1 units: independently executing IronClaw jobs with isolated contexts. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous jobs contend for shared LLM-provider concurrency and available instance resources. - Attenuating coordination relation: a first-party maximum-concurrency envelope and Pending queue prevent all runnable jobs from consuming the shared resource at once. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: queued units remain Pending and become InProgress only after another unit releases capacity. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the queue/cap is explicitly tied to the finite shared capacity that concurrent S1 execution would otherwise overrun; it regulates a concrete cross-unit interference rather than merely choosing message order.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current portfolio of active/pending background work for the IronClaw instance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new work demand, current job state, stalled or no-longer-needed jobs and changing current priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create new work, inspect the full active-job portfolio/status and cancel current Pending/InProgress commitments. - Decision owner: the model-driven IronClaw agent using the standard job-control tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: job repository/state machine, scheduler, createjob, listjobs, jobstatus, canceljob and self-repair state transitions. - Closure path: current job portfolio/state → agent listjobs/jobstatus observation → model current-control decision → create/cancel/continue action → scheduler/job state changes → new portfolio state available to the agent. - Boundary reachability: all four job-control capabilities are documented as built-in tools in the shipped job subsystem and are available to the standard agent path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: scheduler and self-repair enforce mechanics, but the discretionary decision to add or cancel current commitments from the current portfolio is made by the model agent. - Evidence: docs/capabilities/jobs/jobs.mdx, docs/capabilities/jobs/self-repair.mdx. - Basis: explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: timeout/retry and concurrency enforcement are not treated as autonomous S3 ownership. - Whole-system current view: listjobs exposes all active jobs with current state/metadata, with jobstatus providing per-job detail. - Current-control decision scope: create a new commitment or cancel an existing Pending/InProgress job, thereby changing the current work portfolio.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime path establishes an independent complementary audit owner whose distinct operational-reality findings close back into corrective S1 behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S3 at the reviewed runtime boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic authorization/safety gates, logs/audit sinks, self-repair monitoring and repository-development audits exist but do not establish runtime S3 ownership. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: self-repair observes inactivity mechanically and security controls enforce policy; development audits/reviewer agents operate outside the credited product runtime. - Evidence: docs/capabilities/jobs/self-repair.mdx, docs/internal/reborn/target-architecture/ws12-security-audit.md. - Basis: negative after targeted runtime/development-surface review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: audit logging and policy checks may be valuable controls without constituting S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: recognize recurring/future user needs, formulate a persistent automation adaptation and return the parent-approved option into future IronClaw operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated user work, recurring omissions/forgetting, periodic monitoring needs and learned patterns likely to recur. - Decisive decision or feedback right: approve whether the proposed persistent routine becomes part of future operation. - Decision owner: the user as legitimate parent in the shipped routine-advisor mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user/context memory, routine-advisor activation/pattern rules, builtintriggerlist, builtintriggercreate, cron/once schedules and routine execution history. - Closure path: observed recurring pattern/future need → agent proposes specific future automation → user confirms or rejects → on confirmation agent creates the routine → later scheduled/event-driven runs alter future operation. - Boundary reachability: routine-advisor is a shipped first-party skill and explicitly instructs the standard agent to use built-in trigger capabilities after confirmation; no downstream adaptation workflow is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent owns pattern detection and option generation, but the skill explicitly says to always wait for user confirmation. The decisive adaptation judgment therefore remains parent-owned. - Evidence: skills/routine-advisor/SKILL.md, docs/capabilities/routines/cron.mdx. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: dynamic tool discovery/building and current-task extension use are not independently credited as S4 without the prospective distinction; the positive claim rests on the explicit recurring-pattern→future-routine path. - External distinction: recurring user behavior, installed-capability context and expressed/learned needs are evidence outside the current internal execution state. - Future / prospective distinction: the advisor distinguishes tasks likely to recur and proposes a schedule for later executions rather than merely solving the present turn. - Adaptation option generated: a concrete persistent routine specifying what future runs do, when they run and how results are delivered. - Path back into current capability / S3: parent confirmation authorizes builtintriggercreate; the installed routine becomes part of the active operational program and later fires as standard jobs. - Parent ownership of decisive adaptation judgment/feedback right: the shipped skill says to wait for the user to confirm before creating any routine. - Return of parent decision into present capability: confirmation permits the agent to install the trigger immediately. - Subsequent operational/capability change under that returned decision: future cron/event fires execute the newly installed recurring program.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material runtime path establishes an agent or parent closure over IronClaw&#39;s identity or ultimate organizational policy at the declared system boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S5. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user requests, settings, authorization/approval policy, agent name/profile, skills and extension permissions constrain operation but do not by themselves constitute S5 closure. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewed surfaces expose operational authority/configuration and action approvals, not a reconstructable identity/ultimate-policy issue resolved by a legitimate S5 owner and returned as governing identity/policy. - Evidence: README.md, docs/extensions/building-a-tool.md, .env.example. - Basis: negative after targeted policy/configuration review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: human approval of risky tool actions is operational governance; it is not automatically S5=P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/eebe516174b712a144b143da7afd7b636f6e070b/assessments/ironclaw.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit eebe516174b712a144b143da7afd7b636f6e070b\">eebe516 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T15:13:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Artificial-Source/AVA\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AVA</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Artificial-Source/AVA/tree/1b886dc5b80c6bf459cfe47550aa197537d5916d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1b886dc5b80c6bf459cfe47550aa197537d5916d\">1b886dc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously inspect, modify and verify software through model-selected coding/tool actions, directly or in bounded child sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar code, implementation choices, build/test/process failures, tool/provider errors, context pressure, permission boundaries and delegated subtasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive engineering actions, interpret results, decide repairs/delegation and decide when the assigned objective is complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed AVA parent agent and, when instantiated, model-backed general subagents for their delegated outcomes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in tools, permissions, provider transport, session state, task dispatch, context compaction, cancellation and RPC/ACP/TUI hosts. - Closure path: user/parent goal → model chooses repository/tool action or task delegation → first-party runtime executes → evidence/result returns → model revises work until completion or a boundary stops it. - Boundary reachability: ordinary TUI, print and RPC execution instantiate the same first-party agent/tool runtime; task is a standard model-visible tool. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime code transports/constrains actions, but the model owns open-ended engineering selection and interpretation. - Evidence: README.md; src/ava/agent/agentloop.cpp; src/ava/agent/tooldispatchtask.cpp; docs/core/subagents.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: explore is read-only and not counted as a write-capable peer by itself; positive S1 does not require every configured/custom agent to be first-party.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 interference-attenuation loop is established for concurrently active write-capable child agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple general subagents may operate against the same project state, but no standard first-party path was found that owns file/path claims, worktree isolation, peer conflict detection or mutual-adjustment feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: concurrency limits, child ownership, job IDs, recursive-task hiding, permissions and per-session/process synchronization bound execution but do not coordinate peer coding interference. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parent delegation and a maximum concurrent-job count are not enough without a concrete peer-interference relation and attenuation/feedback loop. - Evidence: docs/core/subagents.md; src/ava/agent/subagentcoordinator.cpp; src/ava/agent/subagentlaunch.cpp. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the runtime has extensive locking/process/session safety, but those locks protect runtime state and authority rather than regulating a demonstrated peer-S1 coding collision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current set of delegated operational commitments by observing owner-bound job state and selectively steering or cancelling live child work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a running child that is misdirected, no longer needed, blocked, needs new information, or should be stopped while other current work continues. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect current jobs, request status/result, send a bounded steer to one exact live child, or cancel a selected owned running job. - Decision owner: the model-backed parent AVA agent through the model-visible job tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SubagentCoordinator, BackgroundJobRegistry, exact owner-bound job IDs, FIFO steering queue, cooperative cancellation, completion delivery and live snapshots. - Closure path: parent sees its current job set/status → chooses a particular live commitment → issues job steer/cancel → coordinator delivers the instruction or stop signal to that child → child state/result changes → updated status/completion returns to the parent. - Boundary reachability: model-visible job control is part of the standard first-party subagent runtime; TUI/RPC add promotion/inspection but are not required for the autonomous claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime code enforces ownership and delivery, while the parent model makes the substantive live intervention decision. - Whole-system current view: job list/status exposes the parent&#39;s active/terminal owned child jobs and their current execution state. - Current-control decision scope: selective live-child steer and cancel, plus status/wait/result interpretation; user-facing TUI/RPC can additionally promote eligible work. - Evidence: docs/core/subagents.md; src/ava/agent/jobcontrol.cpp; src/ava/agent/subagentcoordinator.cpp. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic session stop or permission approval is not used for this claim; it rests on selective model-owned current control over the live child-job set.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material standard first-party independent complementary-audit path is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no bundled independent reviewer is assigned a coding claim, direct evidence channel and returned audit judgment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in explore can gather read-only evidence and custom agent definitions can be configured as reviewers, but neither establishes a bundled review function. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: AVA always provides only general and explore; README/configuration explicitly describe reviewer as something configured by the user. Repository-development review history is outside the running-harness boundary. - Evidence: docs/core/subagents.md; docs/core/configuration.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a parent can delegate a review prompt to explore/general or install a reviewer definition, but generic/configured composition is not a standard first-party S3 constructor.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no future/external condition is owned by a function that develops adaptation options and returns them into current organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sessions, compaction, custom context resources, providers/models, plugins/MCP/LSP and resumable history affect later work but are persistence/configuration/current capability surfaces. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: preserving history or loading external capabilities does not demonstrate an outside-and-then option-development owner feeding adaptation into current control. - Evidence: README.md; src/ava/agent/contextcompaction.cpp; src/ava/app/runtime/Session.cpp. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary agents can use web/MCP or persisted context for future-facing work; ad hoc S1 research is not a standard S4 organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level or ultimate-policy conflict is routed to an authoritative S5 owner and returned as governing organizational policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permissions, project trust, tool visibility, configured primary agents/system prompts, containment and RPC/ACP policies constrain execution but do not create identity-policy closure. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents operate inside developer/user-authored policy and cannot authoritatively redefine AVA&#39;s identity or ultimate operating principles. - Evidence: src/ava/permissions/permission.cpp; src/ava/app/projecttrust.cpp; docs/core/configuration.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: permission/trust controls are legitimate authority boundaries, not identity/ultimate-policy governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4cb940736565d1a890a432eeab103fa4cb7cec8a/assessments/ava.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4cb940736565d1a890a432eeab103fa4cb7cec8a\">4cb9407 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T21:00:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xmaks82/self-improving-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">self-improving-agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xmaks82/self-improving-agent/tree/fd59417b91292b48711410a91d2cbf3eaeb0c060\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fd59417b91292b48711410a91d2cbf3eaeb0c060\">fd59417 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: First-party coding loop selecting tools and iterating against source, shell, tests and user requests. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain tasks, code state, tool results, shell failures and model context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose source/tool operations and next coding actions using model observations. - Decision owner: MainAgent model-backed task actor; optional subagents decide scoped tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Client adapters, tool registry, iteration cap, approval, context and guard. - Closure path: tool result → conversation context → next model decision and tool action. - Boundary reachability: CLI constructs MainAgent and bundled tool registry, executes first-party process tool loop; no external coding agent is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A deterministic registry cannot choose the next coding operation without model action selection. - Evidence: src/agent/agents/mainagent.py, src/agent/agents/toolloop.py, src/agent/agents/orchestrator.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Model endpoint is an external reasoning provider, not a borrowed harness. - Boundary reachability: CLI constructs MainAgent and bundled tool registry, executes first-party process tool loop; no external coding agent is required.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No fully evidenced first-party inter-S1 oscillation-reduction cycle at a specific operational recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing child tasks, concurrent improvement runs potentially racing to version prompts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Deterministic background-task guard skips a second improvement run while the first executes; subagent orchestrator gathers task outputs. - Decision owner: Runtime guard enforces same-workflow exclusion; agent decides tasks, but no coordination policy between distinct independent coding S1 operating units was evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: improvementtask guard, async gather and generic mailbox. - Closure path: A duplicate improvement request is reported as skipped; this avoids a version race in one meta-workflow, but does not establish distinct S1 operational-unit feedback stabilization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A guarded single shared critical section in the same improvement pipeline and parallel task decomposition are not by themselves evidence of S2 among separate operating S1s. - Evidence: src/agent/agents/mainagent.py, src/agent/agents/orchestrator.py, src/agent/core/mailbox.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No current whole-organization discretionary resource/priority supervisory loop evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Operation errors, context pressure, budget, agent/model configuration and version quality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The main agent chooses task steps; deterministic limits, approvals and rollback enforce configured thresholds; not a system-wide commitment intervention authority. - Decision owner: Root task agent for coding, user/configuration for limits, deterministic evaluator for thresholds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CostTracker, approval/undo, log manager and conversation compactor. - Closure path: Local operation gating and prompt rollback affect future requests, but not demonstrated whole-system S3 bargaining/accountability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A named orchestrator sequences tasks and agents; it does not demonstrate independent S3 current whole-system control. - Evidence: src/agent/agents/orchestrator.py, src/agent/agents/mainagent.py, src/agent/core/orchestrator.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: An automatic adversarial verifier provides a plausible complementary raw-artifact access path, but audit independence and returned corrective control remain unresolved at this source boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential false claims that code modifications work despite unseen build/test failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: VerificationAgent may independently read files and run build/tests after three edits; a PASS/FAIL/PARTIAL report is given to the user. - Decision owner: A separate verifier persona/model call may judge raw evidence, but it shares the producer tool registry/client and has no evidenced independent mandate or authority to require a subsequent correction. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentPipeline.maybeverify edit counter and call, tool-enabled bounded verifier, VERDICT extraction. - Closure path: Verification report appears in main response; there is no deterministic failure → producer correction → rerun closure; whether operator supervision closes an audit finding in shipped operation cannot be determined. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Unlike generic reviewer names, a separately prompted adversarial agent with first-party direct tests has plausible material S3 access. However the independence and consequential feedback threshold cannot be settled from static source alone. - Evidence: src/agent/agents/pipeline.py, src/agent/agents/verification.py, src/agent/agents/mainagent.py - Basis: structural / unknown - Confidence: medium for reachable audit-like access; low for audit closure. - Caveats: The verifier can fall back to text-only model if tools unsupported, and a failed verifier exception returns None.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Use external user quality feedback and recent interaction histories to form forward-looking improvements to the system prompt, activate a new version and retrospectively rollback failures. - External distinction: Explicit negative or corrective user feedback is new environmental evidence about response fitness outside the harness output channel. - Future / prospective distinction: Analyzer hypothesizes how revised prompt rules would improve later, not just the current, user sessions. - Adaptation option generated: Versioner proposes a validated new active prompt version with targeted model-authored changes. - Path back into current capability / S3: PromptManager switches current.yaml and MainAgent loads that active prompt on the next turn; version feedback can revert failures. This returns the option into operating capability without asserting S3 ownership. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User dissatisfaction, recurring response-quality failures, environmental performance changes across future sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: AnalyzerAgent proposes hypotheses and confidence; VersionerAgent selects prompt modifications, validates and creates a new active prompt version. - Decision owner: First-party separately model-driven AnalyzerAgent and VersionerAgent. Human feedback triggers the review; deterministic threshold prevents low-confidence changes without owning adaptation judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: FeedbackDetector, ImprovementOrchestrator, PromptManager, response logs and version-level metric/rollback checks. - Closure path: negative/confident feedback → analyzer prospective hypotheses → versioner prompt rewrite → PromptManager.createversion switches current.yaml → MainAgent.getsystemprompt uses latest version next turn; feedback metrics may rollback. - Boundary reachability: MainAgent.process wires feedback detection and starts ImprovementOrchestrator in a bounded background task. Prompts live in packaged storage and are loaded afresh for next request; no developer-only CI is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the analyzer/versioner models the same novel option generation and targeted change would not be made by threshold/metadata storage alone. - Evidence: src/agent/agents/mainagent.py, src/agent/agents/mainagent.py, src/agent/core/orchestrator.py, src/agent/agents/analyzer.py, src/agent/agents/versioner.py, src/agent/agents/versioner.py, src/agent/storage/prompts.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for wired prospective capability adaptation; not proven effective empirically. - Caveats: This is feedback-driven future response capability, not automatic model-weight optimization or benchmark superiority. - Boundary reachability: MainAgent.process wires feedback detection and starts ImprovementOrchestrator in a bounded background task. Prompts live in packaged storage and are loaded afresh for next request; no developer-only CI is required.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No explicit ultimate policy/identity issue cycle routed to legitimate higher authority with binding returned system-level decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Risky commands, prompt drift, unwanted self-rewrite and user permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer-set protected meta-agent list, prompt validation rules and operator approvals constrain the system. Their enforcement is not S5 identity adjudication. - Decision owner: Static constructor and human task/operator for local permissions, not a legitimate autonomous S5 ultimate-policy authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: VersionerAgent.PROTECTEDAGENTS, prompt validation, tool security, approval and rollback. - Closure path: Invalid proposed prompt is rejected and task controls enforced; no S5-level issue → authority → policy decision → governed subsequent operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Constraints guarding adaptive S4 from self-poisoning are not an autonomous identity or policy-making loop. - Evidence: src/agent/agents/versioner.py, src/agent/agents/versioner.py, src/agent/storage/prompts.py, src/agent/approval/confirmator.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3120ec0a0f46852f47f2ba59ef4a601a3f17f8c7/assessments/self-improving-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3120ec0a0f46852f47f2ba59ef4a601a3f17f8c7\">3120ec0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T23:58:11+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ownpilot/OwnPilot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OwnPilot</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ownpilot/OwnPilot/tree/eb4c27f29d4d12778621a83e49cd673cd8b62239\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision eb4c27f29d4d12778621a83e49cd673cd8b62239\">eb4c27f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Use tools to achieve a bounded mission or continue a configured monitoring/action mission across cycles. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New data, changing webpages/files, tool failures, provider outputs, user messages and incomplete task results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The Claw model chooses tools, further observations, scripts and completion/retry/reflection in response to actual tool results. - Decision owner: The configured first-party Claw model/tool actor in its supported runtime, using an external inference provider but the packaged Claw execution and feedback integration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool catalog, runner, cycle manager, .claw/ directives, persistent context, tool-result history, time/cost limits and sandbox permissions. - Closure path: Mission → model-chosen tool action → observed tool result → updated session memory/next model choice → report or further cycle. - Boundary reachability: Claw creation and its model/tool runner are part of the installed first-party gateway, not a user-authored hypothetical plugin or stand-alone coding-agent vendor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime schedules and enforces configured bounds but cannot replace the model&#39;s mission-specific action decisions. - Evidence: Claw modes and tool list, Claw runner, subclaw task result. - Basis: Shipped model-directed agent operation with an actual tool/observation loop. - Confidence: High for source wiring, not measured mission success. - Caveats: No live agent/model-provider execution or autonomous effectiveness study is claimed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent concurrent subordinate operational cells from corrupting one another&#39;s workspace or claiming the same pending crew task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parent/child file-state contamination and two active worker agents racing to own one queue task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party subclaw spawning forces child Docker isolation, preventing child writes to its parent&#39;s workspace; atomic crew claim returns exactly one owner and makes other competing agents reselect another task. Policy/queue constraints are configured/enforced by code, not chosen by an S2 manager agent. - Decision owner: The user/parent config supplies the boundary and task source, while first-party S2-specific isolation and claim gates enforce anti-interference; autonomous organizational coordination discretion beyond those paths is not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Child-created docker sandbox, inherited policy, task queue SKIP LOCKED, claimedby ownership, typed result/claim status and sibling messaging tools. - Closure path: Multiple active S1 units contend for shared filesystem or pending work → forced sandbox/transaction isolates outcomes → another agent receives an unsuccessful claim or separate workspace and must use its own next operational action. - Boundary reachability: Child isolation is mandatory in the packaged Claw spawn tool and queue claiming is an exported first-party crew execution tool, both reachable in supported modes without writing anti-contention logic. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The S2 function is concretely anti-interference, not generic routing; C records intentionally exposed first-party mechanism without autonomous ownership of an S2-specific discretionary coordination choice. - Distinct S1 units: Parent Claw and its independently executing child Claws, or two separately configured Soul crew workers. - Inter-S1 disturbance: A child contaminates shared parent mission state through file writes; two workers concurrently claim the same piece of pending crew work. - Attenuating coordination relation: Forced subclaw Docker sandbox independent from parent&#39;s workspace plus a single-winner crew claim transaction. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Conflicting task claims return no task to the losing agent and the parent can inspect child state; isolated child actions cannot overwrite the parent workspace. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The implementation explicitly documents prevention of parent-workspace corruption, and a lock-enforced no-double-claim invariant directly regulates cross-agent competition, unlike task order or messages alone. - Evidence: mandatory isolation purpose and code, atomic task claim, exposed claim tool. - Basis: Narrow first-party mechanism specifically resolves structurally evidenced inter-agent interference, but deterministic gates do not give autonomous S2 discretion. - Confidence: Medium-high; isolated and queue modes have different deployment preconditions. - Caveats: Docker availability matters for subclaws; do not infer broad conflict-free team programming from this bounded witness.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Decide current resource/commitment allocation among child agents executing one parent Claw&#39;s active mission. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing subtask requirements, unfinished children, failed tool runs, wasted effort or no longer relevant subordinate work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The parent model-backed Claw chooses whether/which child missions to create, which mission configuration to pass, inspects child live status and results, and chooses when to stop or redirect its current child work. - Decision owner: The active parent Claw agent, not a queue, fixed cron heartbeat or mere user CRUD dashboard. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Spawn/list/stop executors, actual ClawManager, parentClawId, inherited tool permissions, child state/results, durable session context and tool-observation feedback. - Closure path: Parent mission and currently running child status/results → model decisions to spawn additional specialized work, stop a child or proceed itself → first-party manager changes subordinate execution → resulting status returns to parent on next tool observation. - Boundary reachability: The active runner includes parent-to-child tools, and their handlers require the authentic Claw execution context and instantiate/start/manage children; this is not a user-created coordinator in another repo. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deciding which concurrent work to commit to a child, and changing it after status feedback, is current multi-unit control. Permission and depth guards enforce constraints but are not the owner of the parent&#39;s portfolio choice. - Whole-system current view: The current parent Claw mission, its own plan/history and live states of all getChildClaws(parentId) children, with result/status from completed child calls. - Current-control decision scope: Composition, addition, stop/replacement and outcome integration of the same parent mission&#39;s children; no claim of control over every independent Soul/crew in the OwnPilot installation. - Evidence: parent tool interface, create/start and outcome feedback, complete child inventory and stop. - Basis: Standard agent-owned current allocation across its subordinate S1 missions; parent discretion is separate from deterministic scheduler/policy. - Confidence: Medium-high for code path, no measurement of model ability to choose useful interventions. - Caveats: Optional subclaw spawning is not automatically exercised by every Claw run. No (P) inferred from merely managing cards or user permission toggles.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No sufficiently independent complementary auditor with a closed finding → parent-current-control return is supplied by the standard parent/child Claw hierarchy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parent/child claims may be wrong and tools may fail, but ordinary progress metrics, logs and optional peer requests do not by themselves establish an independent auditor. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No guaranteed independently judging agent with contradictory authority and separate access in the credited Claw mode. requestreview sends a message to a user-selected peer, not an autonomous trusted veto. - Decision owner: The acting Claw itself, optional user-configured peer, or human reader of metrics; no identified first-party S3 holder with independent closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: clawhistory, deterministic run reliability metrics, generic crew requestreview, activity/audit logs and tool-call security guard. - Closure path: Ordinary logs/feedback can inform next work, but there is no default independent claim checking and returned corrective decision into parent allocation from a distinct auditor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A review message or a self-run reliability score does not independently audit organizational reporting. - Evidence: review request is peer message, deterministic run evaluation. - Basis: Bounded negative first-party Claw recursion, without borrowing user-configured independent reviewers. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: Some crews can include a reviewer-role agent; their actual independence and feedback closure must be assessed in a separately pinned configuration.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No demonstrated first-party closed process links externally sensed prospective changes to adoption of new future organizational capabilities for the parent/child mission system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing external AI tools, markets and user environments could require future capabilities; current mission adaptation and learned task recipes only handle known-run history. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No distinct whole-system prospective adaptation owner/return established; users choose extensions, models, identity settings and future missions. - Decision owner: First-party Claw agent may refine its current plan and auto-distill a prior completed task into a skill, but this is not shown to own outside-and-then strategic adaptation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claw reflection, learned skill distillation and lookup, dynamic tool catalog, extensible service and provider discovery. - Closure path: Past task trajectory → model-produced reusable skill → extension installation → future retrieval is operational learning; missing external/future distinction and intentional prospective options/choice for parent capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Replaying skill learned from one&#39;s own task is not equivalent to sensing future environmental challenges and directing sustained capability adaptation. - Evidence: skill distiller, distiller actual installation, autonomy modes. - Basis: Bounded absence of external/prospective organizational adaptation closure. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: Specific self-configured market-research Soul roles could sense the outside, but a feed-to-adoption path is not established at this reviewed hierarchy.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No established first-party settlement of ultimate organizational identity or governing-policy disputes into the parent/child multi-agent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Risks around self-modification, destructive actions, forbidden tools, overspending and mission scope require constraints but are predominantly ordinary operating policy, not ultimate identity-level governance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator configures autonomy policies and tool permissions; no identified first-party identity/policy dispute → legitimate ultimate authority → returned binding decision path. - Decision owner: User for initial identity, mission and safeguards; deterministic permission gate for action allow/deny; the model cannot independently rewrite ultimate mandate under a proven S5 authority mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Autonomy policies, budget limits, immutable Soul core traits and delegated permission gates, optional user approvals, file scopes and allowSubclaws. - Closure path: A forbidden tool is denied or ordinary permission requested, but the system does not turn an ultimate charter/policy disagreement into a resolved organizational rule applied prospectively. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hard permissions and user-defined identity fields, absent a conflict/legitimacy path, do not constitute S5 merely by being configurable. - Evidence: tool permissions, core Soul identity/evolution config. - Basis: Bounded absence of identity/ultimate-policy return loop. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A local operator legitimately defines the assistant&#39;s identity; Methodology S5=P requires a stronger complete governance loop than simple configuration or per-action approval.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4f683648638c0174e62e0cdf1aaa58c5888e7d43/assessments/ow/ownpilot.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4f683648638c0174e62e0cdf1aaa58c5888e7d43\">4f68364 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T17:53:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lukacf/meerkat\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Meerkat</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lukacf/meerkat/tree/ae0517cc7721377c2cf0a58972bdd29cb7f4fbdc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ae0517cc7721377c2cf0a58972bdd29cb7f4fbdc\">ae0517c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-backed operational task execution - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain inputs, tool results, file state and task outcomes - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select tools/actions and final turn outcome - Decision owner: Agent model inside core session - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider runtime, core loop, typed tool dispatch, persistent sessions and runtime drivers - Closure path: Tool outputs and peer results feed subsequent model calls; committed history/artifacts retain the result - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent model inside core session; Architectural availability, not benchmarked task success; standalone and mob-enabled modes are not conflated. - Evidence: crates/meerkat-core/src/agent.rs; README.md agent execution/embedded boundaries; crates/meerkat-mob-mcp/src/agenttools.rs - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Architectural availability, not benchmarked task success; standalone and mob-enabled modes are not conflated. - Boundary reachability: meerkat facade/AgentFactory, core agent loop, runtime session services, agent-side mob tools and host mob controls, optional host-granted WorkGraph namespace and its companion workflow skill. The cited decision path is shipped in this mode and returns through Provider runtime, core loop, typed tool dispatch, persistent sessions and runtime drivers.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Regulate competing agents&#39; durable shared-work claims - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple workers can race for the same ready item or act on stale revision/ownership - Decisive decision or feedback right: Claim/release with exact revision and owner, observe expiry, then reload/reconsider after conflict - Decision owner: Deterministic WorkGraph authority under host namespace grant; autonomous policy/host composition remains explicit - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claim ownership/leases/CAS, readiness service, workflow skill; mob resource-overlap projections are advisory - Closure path: A stale write/claim is rejected; agent reloads current item/snapshot and revises work choice, while release makes work available to another worker - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic WorkGraph authority under host namespace grant; autonomous policy/host composition remains explicit; This is specific claim-contention regulation, not generic DAG/messaging. Host-issued namespace grant and coordinating actor/policy must be composed. Advisory resource claims do not globally enforce exclusivity. - Evidence: docs/guides/workgraph.mdx lines 106–138 and 163–180; crates/meerkat-workgraph/src/lib.rs namespace capability and companion skill; crates/meerkat-workgraph/src/tools.rs; crates/meerkat-mob/src/coordination.rs - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: This is specific claim-contention regulation, not generic DAG/messaging. Host-issued namespace grant and coordinating actor/policy must be composed. Advisory resource claims do not globally enforce exclusivity. - Boundary reachability: meerkat facade/AgentFactory, core agent loop, runtime session services, agent-side mob tools and host mob controls, optional host-granted WorkGraph namespace and its companion workflow skill. The cited decision path is shipped in this mode and returns through Claim ownership/leases/CAS, readiness service, workflow skill; mob resource-overlap projections are advisory. - Distinct S1 units: Concurrent task agents producing separate operational outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Multiple workers can race for the same ready item or act on stale revision/ownership - Attenuating coordination relation: Claim/release with exact revision and owner, observe expiry, then reload/reconsider after conflict - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A stale write/claim is rejected; agent reloads current item/snapshot and revises work choice, while release makes work available to another worker - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: This is specific claim-contention regulation, not generic DAG/messaging. Host-issued namespace grant and coordinating actor/policy must be composed. Advisory resource claims do not globally enforce exclusivity. Regulate competing agents&#39; durable shared-work claims\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Current regulation of the mob&#39;s operational members and commitments - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stalled, failed or costly members and changing team needs - Decisive decision or feedback right: Read whole mob/member current state and token usage; retire unneeded work and spawn/replace profile/model capability; parent can intervene through host controls - Decision owner: Creating/managing agent in base mode; legitimate local mob operator in parent mode - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent moblistmembers/check/retire/spawn tools, ownership scopes and host RPC/CLI/SDK status/cancel/lifecycle APIs - Closure path: Agent inspects status then changes roster/commitments; retirement removes recipient authority and new spawn changes subsequent work; host decisions enter the same runtime lifecycle - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Creating/managing agent in base mode; legitimate local mob operator in parent mode; Not credited from static budgets or supervisor naming. Scope is the managed mob or owned descendant team; arbitrary host-only rights are not attributed to agents. - Evidence: crates/meerkat-mob-mcp/src/agenttools.rs lines 2840–2980; docs/concepts/mobs.mdx host versus agent boundaries; docs/guides/mobs.mdx operator observation/lifecycle; crates/meerkat-mob/src/controlpolicy.rs - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Not credited from static budgets or supervisor naming. Scope is the managed mob or owned descendant team; arbitrary host-only rights are not attributed to agents. - Boundary reachability: meerkat facade/AgentFactory, core agent loop, runtime session services, agent-side mob tools and host mob controls, optional host-granted WorkGraph namespace and its companion workflow skill. The cited decision path is shipped in this mode and returns through Agent moblistmembers/check/retire/spawn tools, ownership scopes and host RPC/CLI/SDK status/cancel/lifecycle APIs. - Whole-system current view: The cited current state/report path covers all units in the declared deployment. - Current-control decision scope: Read whole mob/member current state and token usage; retire unneeded work and spawn/replace profile/model capability; parent can intervene through host controls\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No supplied complementary independent corrective audit loop - Disturbance / variety regulated: Accuracy of agent completion reports - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party audit-specific judgment/access/return topology established - Decision owner: None established at the shipped runtime boundary - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Council/fork helpers, status/history, tool authorization audit records, structured output checks and developer TLC/tests - Closure path: Councils are generic peer collaboration and records expose operational provenance; no packaged independently governed corrective audit loop is established - Why this is / is not agent-owned: None established at the shipped runtime boundary; Independent reviewer examples, development validators and routine artifact checking do not supply assessed-runtime S3. - Evidence: docs/reference/builtin-tools.mdx council/forks; crates/meerkat-mob-mcp/src/temporarycouncil.rs; crates/meerkat-core/src/authorization/audit.rs; README.md lifecycle verification scope - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective capability adaptation loop supplied - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing future environment or capability needs - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped environmental sensing/options/operational-capability return owner established - Decision owner: None established - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Adaptive layer planning, model fallback/brainswap, tool catalog selection, memory, skill updates and schedules - Closure path: Adaptive decisions select next internal task layer from previous results; fallback and loading regulate present execution, without a first-party external/future adaptation loop - Why this is / is not agent-owned: None established; Adaptive nomenclature, dynamic tools and a research-team example are not an S4-specific constructor or parent adaptation loop. - Evidence: crates/meerkat-mob/src/adaptive.rs LayerDecision/PlanningTurnRequest/run loop; docs/reference/builtin-tools.mdx; docs/concepts/memory-and-compaction.mdx - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop supplied - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ultimate purpose/policy conflict of the deployed system - Decisive decision or feedback right: No issue-to-legitimate-authority-to-returned-ultimate-policy closure established - Decision owner: Host/operator config authority alone is not a qualifying S5 owner - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Realm auth/config, grants, signed mobpack trust, skills identity migrations and approval records - Closure path: Controls enforce host-selected boundaries; skill UUID migration is identifier administration, and approval records require effect-policy composition - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Host/operator config authority alone is not a qualifying S5 owner; MobKit governance mirrors and internal governed-default ADRs are adjacent. Legitimate operator current control is S3, not automatic S5. - Evidence: docs/reference/skills-governance-runbook.mdx; crates/meerkat-mob/src/controlpolicy.rs; README.md approvals; docs/concepts/mobs.mdx - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: MobKit governance mirrors and internal governed-default ADRs are adjacent. Legitimate operator current control is S3, not automatic S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4e1e5f4c20edc218545e68c1a8b95b08d6b9a457/assessments/me/meerkat.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4e1e5f4c20edc218545e68c1a8b95b08d6b9a457\">4e1e5f4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T23:09:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/codewhale-hq/Codewhale\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Codewhale</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/codewhale-hq/Codewhale/tree/327c15df976601ca3114ea40ac3dc88cea9f152a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 327c15df976601ca3114ea40ac3dc88cea9f152a\">327c15d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: inspect, modify and validate a user&#39;s codebase or workspace through a model-directed tool/action loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: arbitrary coding tasks, changing repository content, tool errors, test feedback and model output variation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the instantiated Codewhale agent chooses file edits, commands and corrective steps from observed results. - Decision owner: first-party model/tool loop in the CLI/Runtime; hosted/local inference endpoints generate replies but are not owners of Codewhale&#39;s tool authorization or run lifecycle. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, permission/sandbox checks, file revisions, transcript/session memory and provider transport. - Closure path: task prompt → model/tool decision → read/edit/command execution → observed diff/test result → next agent action or final validated artifact. - Boundary reachability: codewhale exec and interactive Work mode are documented everyday product entry points with a shipped Rust engine and tool set. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model-driven actor the CLI could still enforce permissions or run commands manually, but would not autonomously select coding work and repair steps. - Evidence: README; MODES; engine; agent tool. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate simultaneous child editors&#39; interference in shared workspace files and declared coordination resources. - Distinct S1 units: separate model-driven child workers/fleet roles executing parallel coding tasks, each with a possible write envelope. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping parallel write scopes, conflicting edits on the same file/contract, stale shared checkout state and duplicate incompatible ownership claims. - Attenuating coordination relation: Runtime requires declared exact files/write roots or coordination contracts, checks active overlaps before mutation, or launches truly isolated worktrees. Workflow validation rejects parallel writable tasks with intersecting scopes, while manager/worker receipts permit later non-conflicting merge/handoff. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: an overlapping claim is refused instead of permitting simultaneous conflicting writes; a selected isolated worktree executes separately and returns branch/artifact identity for manager-mediated integration. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: a concrete same-file/contract contention witness is checked by a specific overlap/exclusion relation that directly changes whether independently active S1 workers can proceed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: write contention and destructive workspace interference among parallel S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: validate/admit or block conflicting writer claims and return separation into later work. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic write-scope/worktree coordination constructor with operator/manager-provided scope; broader autonomous conflict trade-off remains to be composed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live claim registry, path normalization, authorization ceilings, git worktrees, task/role status and ledger receipts. - Closure path: parallel work claim → active/shared scope comparison → reject or isolated admission → permitted edits/worker result → coordinator selects a safe follow-on handoff. - Boundary reachability: SUBAGENTS, Workflow overlap check and source subagent tools are first-party standard multiworker paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: overlap exclusion is specific S2 capacity but its decisive arbitration is fixed policy plus caller claims, not a mandatory model-owned negotiation/repair owner; hence C, not A. - Evidence: SUBAGENTS; Workflow lib; fleet/Runtime. - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise the current user coding mission and subordinate worker commitments/results until completion, correction, or explicit blocked return. - Whole-system current view: at the declared workspace mission boundary, the manager receives child status, task/receipt/validation outcomes, durable goal state and work artifacts, not every unrelated local process. - Current-control decision scope: when to delegate/fan-in, retry, inspect/verify, continue after a failed attempt, stop/escalate or report terminal goal status. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task failure, stale child result, incomplete verification, workflow dependency blockage and partial goal progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-driven manager/goal agent chooses corrective child actions and marks evidence-backed notachieved, complete or blocked states; Runtime and bounded ledger enforce those choices. - Decision owner: first-party main model agent/manager in the agent-authored Workflow or persistent goal continuation mode; not the deterministic worker scheduler. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fleet task ledger and leases, workflow plan/role gates, status/heartbeat, terminal receipts, creategoal/updategoal tools and bounded retry budgets. - Closure path: manager plan/current goal → child tasks and tools → wait/receipt/evidence → agent-selected retry/rework/continuation → updated goal/status and later work or terminal result. - Boundary reachability: FLEET manager-owned fan-in, source-distributed goal tools, fleet manager and runtime threads close configured supported paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model interprets varied child/goal evidence and chooses next mission interventions; removing it leaves queueing and receipt bookkeeping, not comparable discretionary mission supervision. - Evidence: FLEET; goal.rs; fleet manager. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: S3 is at one manager-owned mission recursion, not all repositories or installed Codewhale sessions. A scheduled task alone is not S3 ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: challenge a producer&#39;s output using separately routed reviewer/verifier roles and gate consequential rework before downstream progression. - Claim being audited: an implementation artifact/diff is correct, validated and ready for the next role or final completion. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer/child produces diff, artifacts, logs, model self-report and terminal task receipt. - Complementary access path: Workflow Review and Verify gates accept explicit findings/verdicts from separately configured reviewer/verifier role routes; the pinned digest/route receipts and lane-scoped handoff distinguish the review artifact from the implementer&#39;s final prose. - Independence boundary: reviewer and verifier may be distinct fleet agents/models with frozen exact route receipts, but independence is composition-dependent. A bare ReviewRepairLoop data structure alone is not an automatically spawned auditor. - Who acts on findings: gate evaluation blocks the downstream role or triggers bounded upstream retry/escalation; a manager/developer must wire the reviewer actor and corrective return, with optional parent ratification. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false completion, unreviewed diffs, stale review against moved artifacts, missing/verifier receipts and unsupported pass claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reviewer can issue substantive RepairsNeeded/failed/inconclusive judgment and withhold advancement; the gate consumes it to request rework or escalation. - Decision owner: a separately configured model-driven reviewer/verifier and first-party lane gate/ReviewRepairLoop; assembling a truly independent reviewer and correction loop is not universal/default. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: exact provider/model route receipts, input digest, bounded iterations/time/tools, explicit verdict and fail-closed status, optional human ratification. - Closure path: producer artifact → separately routed reviewer/verifier evidence → gate verdict → retry/block/escalate to producer/manager → changed artifact/digest and subsequent verification → accepted downstream release or honest blocked status. - Boundary reachability: Workflow gates, reviewrepair.rs, documented FLEET/Workflow roles are source-distributed enabled compositions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewer judgment can be a distinct model decision; the controller does not itself supply that independent actor unconditionally, so C rather than unconditional A. - Evidence: gates.rs; reviewrepair.rs; FLEET. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-low - Caveats: role and digest records strengthen provenance but do not prove independent reviewer execution in every run. Tests exercised the IR semantics, not a live provider reviewer in this assessment.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party prospective organizational adaptation control/return loop established beyond current coding task goals and configurable runtime options. - Disturbance / variety regulated: evolving providers, future repository requirements, model quality/cost and role fit were checked as candidates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no owner evidenced selecting future mission capability/policy changes from environmental trends and closing them into later system operation as a separate organizational function. - Decision owner: user configures fleet/model routes/goals, and main agent repairs current task; those choices are not a distinct S4 organ at the reviewed recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model catalog, Reasoning Router, role profiles, durable sessions, goals and workflow plans. - Closure path: goal updates/rework improve current mission outcome; there is no source-backed external future distinctions → adaptation alternatives → selected capability change → later effectiveness feedback circuit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: durable memory, dynamic model selection and iterative verification can serve S1/S3 without establishing outside-and-then adaptation. - Evidence: FLEET; MODES; goal.rs. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no independent legitimate ultimate-identity/policy adjudication and return loop evidenced within a workspace run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicting permissions, safety policy, user role settings and approval requests are already governed by configured authority ceilings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no agent/runtime right to redefine the organization&#39;s ultimate purpose/identity beyond ordinary user-specified coding goals and policy constraints. - Decision owner: user/administrator chooses policy stack and project/workspace trust; Runtime enforces non-escalating delegated authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission postures, sandbox, tool ACLs, worktree claims, delegated scope intersection and explicit approvals. - Closure path: approve/deny or config changes constrain later tasks, but are not an unresolved identity/policy conflict → authoritative deliberation → returned S5 governance loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: personal skill/persona profiles and user authorization are not ultimate-policy S5 decisions for the coding organization. - Evidence: AUTHORIZATIONORDER; MODES; SUBAGENTS. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/15898f7e0bc020dba12821320166ba7ccaae4122/assessments/co/codewhale.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 15898f7e0bc020dba12821320166ba7ccaae4122\">15898f7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T18:36:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SuperagenticAI/superqode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SuperQode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SuperagenticAI/superqode/tree/5284b049e5e39e3159ca1a178ac4c655a3305a26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5284b049e5e39e3159ca1a178ac4c655a3305a26\">5284b04 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform open-ended coding/inspection work with first-party model-steered code, file, tool and observation loops. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown repo state, tool errors, changing files and nontrivial task progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select task tools, arguments, follow-up steps and completion based on results. - Decision owner: model-driven actor inside SuperQode&#39;s first-party AgentLoop; model service is an inference dependency. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: gateway, tool registry/executor, policies, harness session, native RLM/PiPy modes, journal, checkpoint and WorkOrder native-worker configuration. - Closure path: task → model output → tool calls / native executor → result observations → next model request or final task result. A WorkOrder task repeats this S1 pattern for each configured native worker. - Boundary reachability: shipped native agent loop and native RLM/PiPy backend are reachable through installed CLI/harness runtime, without invoking a separate vendor coding agent. Credit is bounded to these first-party paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: next engineering actions are chosen by a model within the native tool loop instead of a predetermined script; safety limits do not supply the model&#39;s decisions. - Evidence: src/superqode/agent/loop.py, src/superqode/harness/kernel.py, src/superqode/harness/backends/rlm.py, src/superqode/workorders/runner.py. - Basis: source-backed native loop and bundled runtime. - Confidence: high for first-party structure, not a live model task benchmark. - Caveats: external ACP/vendor routes are excluded from S1 attribution, and RLM/PiPy may require configured providers/dependencies.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: first-party constructor for conflict-specific attenuation between distinct native WorkOrder coding-worker S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two isolated WorkOrder workers producing overlapping/conflicting patches for shared integration state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: detect changed-path/write integration collision, reject competing integration and change the affected task from running to blocked so it cannot silently progress. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party WorkOrder integration/store controller owns the conflict gate; a parent may later choose remediation and restart. This is not autonomous model negotiation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-task worktrees and baselines, integration lock, concurrent changed-path comparison, blocked task and explicit parent resume. - Closure path: independent native worker task result → integratetaskworkspace compares patch files against concurrent changes → conflict list → store.blocktask and work-order BLOCKED gate → conflict exposed in work status / cockpit → parent may adjust/retry the affected worker task. No uncontrolled auto-merge on conflict. - Boundary reachability: supported superqode work run/worker uses executeclaimedtask which calls integration logic for patch roles when WorkOrder has task-scoped worktree isolation; all code is first-party and in the installed WorkOrder mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the conflict-specific coordination relation is implemented, but decision/repair relies on deterministic conflict gating and parent operation rather than an independent autonomous S2 agent; thus C, not A. - Evidence: src/superqode/workorders/runner.py, src/superqode/workorders/integration.py, src/superqode/workorders/store.py, src/superqode/workorders/cockpit.py. - Distinct S1 units: separate first-party WorkOrder native coding tasks with independent run/worker IDs and task-local worktrees. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping modifications to the same integration candidate/base tree. - Attenuating coordination relation: serialized integration and baseline/path collision check with blocked admission for conflicting patches. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: block persists as task state; the worker cannot continue as accepted; parent sees conflict and may repair and resume/requeue the blocked task for a later attempt. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: actual cross-task overlapping writes are checked against concurrent state and alter the admissibility of a worker&#39;s contribution, rather than merely limiting concurrency or exchanging results. - Basis: explicit shipped first-party conflict-to-block path. - Confidence: medium-high for optional isolated WorkOrder mode. - Caveats: deterministic construction with parent resolution; non-isolated/single-worker chat or external-agent routes do not provide this S2 claim.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: parent-governed current control of an entire configured WorkOrder&#39;s operational commitments, constraints and blocked/accepted execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: worker stalls, resource/budget exhaustion, patch conflict, reviewer veto and uncertain readiness of an order-wide integration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: after whole-order cockpit/status evidence, operator can set/revise work task commitments and constraints, resume specific blocked work, stop/cancel an order, or approve/reject a prepared candidate; controller enforces the return. - Decision owner: legitimate parent operator controlling the WorkOrder via superqode work CLI; not the default model or mere code-tool approval. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: work status/tree/usage/watch, ordered task states/dependencies/budget, WorkOrderStore.resume/cancel/accept, conflict/approval gates and journalled decisions. - Closure path: order-wide current status (tasks/workers/conflicts/budget/reviews) → human operational decision via work resume/cancel/add-task/queue/approve/reject → WorkOrder store transition → worker scheduling and acceptance change on next operation. - Boundary reachability: bundled superqode work commands operate on the first-party durable WorkOrder store; cockpit exposes whole-system state. Parent decisions are implemented in store actions and subsequent worker dispatch; no external organization control plane is imported. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: this qualifies solely as parent mode P, not autonomous A or constructor C. A generic tool approval alone would not suffice; evidence is the order-wide current-state observation and distinct parent control actions that change ongoing task commitments. - Evidence: src/superqode/commands/work.py, src/superqode/workorders/store.py, src/superqode/workorders/cockpit.py, src/superqode/workorders/runner.py. - Whole-system current view: order status, individual worker leases and tasks, conflicts, remaining budget, policy and review/check gates and integration decision in cockpit. - Current-control decision scope: assign additional tasks/constraints at the WorkOrder level; stop/restore blocked operational flow and accept/reject controlled integration under budget/risk. - Basis: parent-governed current WorkOrder decision/return evidence. - Confidence: moderate; mode-specific to WorkOrder parent CLI. - Caveats: do not transfer this S3 parent authority to a native standalone coding turn, static workflow or external vendor harness.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: a configured native WorkOrder reviewer acts independently of its producer coding worker to test a code-change acceptance claim against actual integration state and dependency evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: producer may claim a patch is correct while resulting code and checks reveal defects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-driven reviewer child owns a distinct code-correctness verdict, which the WorkOrder controller requires before integration can progress. - Decision owner: separate first-party REVIEWER task executing its own harness run, not the producing worker&#39;s in-band summary; first-party controller checks the verdict and blocks on nonapproval. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WorkTaskRole.REVIEWER prompt, separate task/workspace scope, evidence-only mutation check, SUPERQODEREVIEW JSON parsing, stored review artifact and integration approval precondition. - Closure path: earlier coding task/integrated source state and dependency evidence → configured separate reviewer native run → structured approved/changesrequested evidence → enforcerolecontract returns BLOCK for failure or nonapproval → prepareintegration rejects missing/nonapproved reviewer verdict → parent observes remedy/block; successful review allows separate checks/parent merge decision. - Boundary reachability: superqode work add-task --role reviewer / task creation and bundled rununtilidle execute configured reviewer tasks through first-party harness runner; taskprompt requests examination of integrated state; enforcement exists in installed WorkOrder and integration code. No uninstalled development test or stand-alone CI is used. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewer model owns substantive code-quality adjudication within a distinct S1-independent role/task and returns findings through explicit controller gate; guard itself is deterministic but does not replace reviewer judgment. - Evidence: src/superqode/workorders/models.py, src/superqode/workorders/runner.py, src/superqode/workorders/integration.py, src/superqode/commands/work.py. - Claim being audited: correctness/acceptability of integrated code produced by coding worker(s), not the generic model&#39;s own done text. - Ordinary reporting path: producer task run result and self-reported completion with artifacts and tool output. - Complementary access path: reviewer role reads integrated repository state and predecessor evidence in a separate task, with evidence-only file guard. - Independence boundary: distinct worker/role/task and workspace scope, separate native harness run, reviewer cannot become author by changing files without a violation. The same external inference provider may still be configured; this is operational independence of roles, not an independently attested external reviewer. - Who acts on findings: WorkOrder controller blocks task/completion or refuses integration when verdict is negative/missing; parent can inspect outcomes and decide correction/resume. - Basis: explicit first-party reviewer task + binding integration-gate path. - Confidence: moderate for configured native WorkOrder reviewer; live reviewer decision results were not independently exercised. - Caveats: positive A is mode-specific and source-backed, not proof default chat always audits, not an independent organizational assessment or paper-grade S3 audit. Merely configured vendor reviewer without a native first-party actor is excluded.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: parent-governed prospective change of installed HarnessSpec capability based on external/held-out workload evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing task distributions, regressions or inadequate model/harness capability relative to evaluation tasks outside a single production prompt. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator chooses candidate evaluation, staging/canary, activation or rollback based on independent held-out/test evidence and policy rather than the coding model autonomously rewriting its own spec. - Decision owner: parent operator with required actor identity; evaluation and policy machinery constrain the choice, while no autonomous S4 owner is demonstrated. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: harness eval, optimize-omni, candidate audit/held-out gates, staged snapshot, canary %, promotion policy, activateharnesspromotion and rollback. - Closure path: external held-out tasks/eval results → candidate HarnessSpec with audit → parent command stage/canary/activate and policy approval → atomic candidate copy to base operational HarnessSpec → subsequent sessions use changed capability; rollback can restore original. - Boundary reachability: installed superqode harness optimize-omni and harness promote CLI commands wire to first-party candidate audit and activation modules; actual base spec copy is a committed runtime configuration change rather than mere advice. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: decisive future/capability adaptation is parent-controlled P. Built-in optimizer/eval/GEPA facilities supply candidate evidence; they do not establish that an autonomous S4 agent owns the promotion. - Evidence: src/superqode/commands/harness/optimization.py, src/superqode/commands/harness/promotion.py, src/superqode/harness/promotion.py, src/superqode/harness/eval.py. - External distinction: held-out workload/evaluation evidence rather than the same current coding tool result. - Future / prospective distinction: selecting whether a candidate HarnessSpec should replace the base for future tasks. - Adaptation option generated: candidate spec from optimized/modified harness choices. - Path back into current capability / S3: staged→canary→policy-gated activated spec copied to the base; parent decision changes the subsequently runnable harness. - Basis: first-party promotion state machine and user-governed action path. - Confidence: medium-high that optional parent mode exists, without claim it was exercised live during this assessment. - Caveats: user invocation, evaluation input and operator authorization are required; optimization research artifacts alone are not S4.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no supported distinct first-party rightful ultimate organizational identity/policy judgment loop above WorkOrder/current controls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsafe tools, budget, promotion risk and authorizations can be constrained by policies but are not identity-purpose conflicts resolved by S5. - Decisive decision or feedback right: adjudicate who the overall organizational system is, what ultimate purposes/policies prevail and return authoritative identity/policy decisions to S3/S4; not demonstrated. - Decision owner: project/operator supplies configuration and governance rules, first-party engine enforces them; WorkOrder operator actions provide S3 and HarnessSpec promotion S4, not identity-level policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: superqode.governance, user approvals, policy engine, budget/cap and promotion authorization. - Closure path: existing configured rules can block unsafe requests/promotion; no higher first-party S5 identity decision and return loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: centralized allow/deny configuration and a human merge/promotion decision do not automatically constitute a legitimate ultimate organizational-purpose adjudicator. - Evidence: src/superqode/governance.py, src/superqode/harness/kernel.py, src/superqode/harness/promotion.py, src/superqode/commands/work.py. - Basis: bounded negative after checking plausible parent and first-party paths. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: external adopter/maintainer organization may own S5, but it is outside this installed system assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/54728d1b516605de7c57abe2f976de67e142e8a6/assessments/superqode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 54728d1b516605de7c57abe2f976de67e142e8a6\">54728d1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-22T21:21:46+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/preloop/preloop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Preloop</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-22\">2026-09-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/preloop/preloop/tree/06d53f902d9a8712ea3345ae0bee9b0b8292f917\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 06d53f902d9a8712ea3345ae0bee9b0b8292f917\">06d53f9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform bounded coding, review, security, evidence-collection and other agent work against an admitted objective through a governed external runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous tracker events and user objectives, repository/environment state, tool/model feedback, failures, review/CI feedback and task-local uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive reasoning path, tool calls and task actions within the objective and Preloop policy/budget envelope. - Decision owner: the autonomous external model/agent actor launched or governed by Preloop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MCP firewall, model gateway, managed credentials, flow execution/runner lifecycle, tool allowlists, budgets, approvals, workspace/checkpoint recovery, runtime plugins and session observability. - Closure path: objective/event is admitted → Preloop starts or governs an agent runtime → the model chooses actions/tools → Preloop mediates calls and returns environment results → the model continues until a bounded result/commit/review/evidence outcome is produced. - Boundary reachability: the frozen README documents onboarding existing agents and launching event/CI-triggered flows through the self-hosted service; supported runtime adapters and private runners reach those external actors directly without relying on repository-development CI. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Preloop decides admission and enforces constraints, but substantive task-local reasoning and action selection belong to the external model actor. - Evidence: README.md; docs/guide/flows/automated-issue-implementation.md; docs/guide/flows/flow-delegation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: no internal VSM function of Claude Code, Codex, Cursor or another governed runtime is inherited merely because Preloop launches or mediates it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no installation-level S2 mutual-adjustment function is established from the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Preloop can run many sessions/flows and maintains lineage, concurrency, budgets and notes, but the inspected paths do not establish a distinct installation-level oscillation/interference among sibling S1 units together with a first-party relation whose organizational purpose is to attenuate that interaction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at the installation recursion. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child-flow lineage, fan-out/depth/tree budgets, runner concurrency, transactional admission locking, sendnote, waiting/resume and stop propagation structure work and resource use but are not credited as S2 without the required disturbance witness. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no installation-level S2 function was established, so there is no S2 decision right to classify. - Evidence: docs/guide/flows/flow-delegation.md; docs/guide/operator-notes.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: delegation and communication can be ingredients in a downstream S2 design, but generic routing, shared budgets, sequencing and notes do not manufacture the function on their own.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: regulate current account-wide agent activity, access and resource commitments using live agent/session/flow/spend/policy state and intervention controls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: active sessions and executions can consume excessive budget, take a wrong operational direction, continue under unsafe tool/model access, or require an immediate account-wide or targeted stop/steer decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose current intervention — change policy/budget/access, steer a live agent, stop an execution, or halt/recover gateway/tool/flow scopes — in response to current installation conditions. - Decision owner: base constructor mode — no resident autonomous installation supervisor is supplied; a downstream controller can be composed over the first-party API/CLI/state/intervention surfaces. Parent mode — the authorized installation/account operator, approver or administrator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime-session timelines, managed-agent/flow inventory, spend attribution, policy engine, approval service, operator-note delivery, execution stop intents, account kill-switch transactions, gateway/tool enforcement and worker recovery/confirmation. - Closure path: current account/session/flow/spend/policy state is observed → controller/operator selects a targeted steer/policy/budget/stop or account halt/recovery action → first-party API/CLI writes that decision → gateway/firewall/scheduler/worker paths enforce it → subsequent current operation is steered, admitted, denied, stopped or resumed under the returned decision. - Boundary reachability: the self-hosted distribution exposes account/session/flow state plus operator-note, policy, approval, budget and kill-switch actions through its console, CLI and HTTP APIs. These are runtime surfaces, not repository-maintainer controls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic policies, budgets and kill-switch propagation enforce current decisions but do not decide when policy should change or an emergency intervention should occur. The standard product deliberately exposes the S3-specific observation/intervention path without assigning that installation-wide discretion to a resident autonomous agent. - Evidence: README.md; docs/guide/operator-notes.md; docs/guide/account-kill-switch.md; docs/guide/flows/flow-delegation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: lower-recursion parent agents can autonomously delegate, inspect descendants and send bounded notes, but that does not promote their local management to installation-wide S3 ownership. - Whole-system current view: the control plane maintains account-scoped managed-agent/session/flow state, live timelines and spend/policy information, while the kill-switch status exposes the current account-wide gateway/tools/flows halt state. The function boundary is the governed account rather than every external system an agent may reach. - Current-control decision scope: current policy/budget/access changes, live steering notes, execution stop control and account-wide gateway/tools/flows halt/recovery.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge an implementation/PR claim against fresh code, issue and review evidence and return trusted findings into corrective implementation work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementer may claim completion while the produced change contains defects, security/quality regressions, unmet requirements or current-head CI/review failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently judge the produced PR/change, issue review findings or a review action, and thereby supply corrective feedback that can trigger the next bounded repair execution. - Decision owner: the separate autonomous model actor executing the Pull Request Reviewer flow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: distinct reviewer flow/conversation, bounded PR diff and referenced-issue reads, stateful findings, trusted reviewer actor IDs, provider/current-head gates, runner-controlled verification evidence, feedback debounce/coalescing, branch binding, checkpoints and fresh repair execution budgets/credentials. - Closure path: implementer publishes or updates a PR → separate reviewer flow reads PR/diff/issue evidence and posts findings/review action → durable feedback authenticates configured reviewer identity and current PR/head → feedback is reserved into a fresh implementation repair execution → implementer resumes the bound branch/conversation and addresses findings → later current-head review/CI state is reconciled again. - Boundary reachability: both Pull Request Reviewer and Automated Issue Implementation/durable-feedback modes are shipped first-party presets and runtime services; the reviewer/repair connection is available through normal flow configuration rather than repository-development CI for Preloop itself. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the critical review judgment is produced by a separate model-driven reviewer conversation, not by the implementation agent or a deterministic gate. Provider and runner checks constrain trusted evidence but do not replace reviewer judgment. - Evidence: docs/guide/flows/pull-request-review.md; backend/presets/002-pull-request-reviewer.yaml; docs/guide/flows/durable-implementation-feedback.md; docs/guide/flows/automated-issue-implementation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human approval by itself is not credited as S3. The positive claim depends on the distinct autonomous reviewer plus authenticated corrective return; deterministic trusted verification is supporting complementary evidence rather than the sole owner. - Claim being audited: the implementation agent&#39;s produced PR/change and its implicit or explicit claim that the requested change is correct and ready under the relevant review criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: the implementer writes its result/commit and Preloop publishes/binds the PR and implementation execution state. - Complementary access path: the reviewer independently fetches the current PR metadata/diff/comments and referenced issue and may inspect bounded project context; runner/provider gates independently read current-head checks and review state. - Independence boundary: reviewer and implementer are separate flows/conversations; durable feedback distinguishes trustedreviewerids from implementeractorids, ignores untrusted/copied review markers, and creates each repair as a new execution with fresh credentials/budgets. - Who acts on findings: the implementation thread receives trusted review/CI feedback in the next repair execution and the autonomous implementer acts on it; unresolved blockers can instead remain visible for human action.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: sense external model-provider tariff changes, generate a verified future pricing-policy adaptation and return an approved reviewed feed into serving Preloop processes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider models, regional tariffs, cache policies, effective dates and pricing evidence change outside the installation, making existing cost estimates stale or incomplete. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether the externally evidenced candidate pricing revision becomes an approved publication consumed by the installation. - Decision owner: the human/operator who reviews and merges/publishes the pricing change; the scheduled review agent prepares and tests the option but does not own final adoption. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: public weekly model-price-review preset, provider/public evidence reads, reviewed catalog and manifest, isolated verification/publication tooling, revision/provenance/expiry validation, allowlists, periodic reviewed-feed polling and atomic runtime price-map replacement. - Closure path: scheduled review examines external provider pricing evidence → agent prepares catalog/manifest/feed changes and tested PR → human reviews and merges/publishes the revision → configured serving processes poll the reviewed artifact → validated price policy replaces current estimates without application deployment → future model-cost control uses the adapted prices. - Boundary reachability: the weekly review preset and reviewed-feed consumer are documented first-party distribution surfaces at the frozen revision. The mode requires normal operator configuration and a human publication decision, not Preloop repository maintainer machinery as an implicit hidden owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the autonomous review agent performs sensing and option generation, but the documented contract explicitly says human PR merge controls feed publication. The decisive adaptation right therefore remains parent-governed. - Evidence: docs/guide/model-price-refresh.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic saved-session history, search and replay are not S4. The positive mapping relies specifically on external tariff sensing plus a documented adaptation option and return into runtime pricing. - External distinction: official/public model-provider pricing evidence and regional/cache tariff information are outside Preloop and can change independently of the installation. - Future / prospective distinction: the weekly scheduled review prepares new or future-effective pricing revisions before subsequent governed requests are costed, including explicitly future-dated tariff policies where supported. - Adaptation option generated: the review flow prepares catalog/manifest/generated-feed changes in a tested PR with provenance, effective dates and unresolved-evidence reporting. - Path back into current capability / S3: after parent approval/publication, the reviewed-feed service polls the artifact and atomically replaces serving-process price maps; subsequent current budget/cost regulation uses the updated estimates.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established for the installed Preloop organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: policy-as-code, roles, approvals, budgets, kill switches, flow configuration and security controls regulate what current/adaptive work may do, but the inspected distribution does not identify an identity/ultimate-policy issue resolved by an ultimate authority and returned as a governing organizational decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as S5. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: account roles, YAML/CEL tool policy, approvals, model/tool allowlists, budget configuration, kill switch, flow configuration and human merge gates are operational governance mechanisms rather than demonstrated S5 closure. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no S5 function was established to classify; the presence of parent authority over operational controls does not by itself create identity/ultimate-policy governance. - Evidence: README.md; docs/guide/account-kill-switch.md; docs/guide/flows/durable-implementation-feedback.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human-controlled policy and emergency authority are materially important governance, but S5 requires a distinct identity/ultimate-policy function rather than generic high-privilege administration.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/adb765db8190634e8deec410a583bab6cfa955ee/assessments/preloop.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit adb765db8190634e8deec410a583bab6cfa955ee\">adb765d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T19:45:11+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tao12345666333/ankaloop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AnkaLoop</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tao12345666333/ankaloop/tree/6a4aec4b258d4980dad4d8ea0e455ba5d6cd2216\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6a4aec4b258d4980dad4d8ea0e455ba5d6cd2216\">6a4aec4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: carry out open-ended coding tasks through model-driven file/tool execution and observation feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing project files, code search, failed commands, tool errors, user instructions and child task results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses executable tools/arguments, subsequent corrections, task delegation and completion based on observations. - Decision owner: first-party AnkaLoop Agent model-directed actor with tool-loop mediator and locally registered tools; selected external model supplies inference. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolLoop, tool registry, ToolExecutor, session state, asynchronous provider adapters, native file/shell tools and optional child task. - Closure path: request→callllm→model-chosen tools→local/MCP ToolExecutor.execute→tool observation appended to model context→follow-up action or terminal answer. - Boundary reachability: ordinary CLI/server/Telegram package launch invokes this first-party runtime without an external coding-agent harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model steers context-dependent coding work, not a fixed deterministic script. - Evidence: src/ankaloop/toolloop.py, src/ankaloop/agent.py, src/ankaloop/toolexecution.py, README.md. - Basis: source-evidenced first-party feedback loop. - Confidence: high for integrated path; not live runtime performance. - Caveats: provider access, model quality and optional external tools not independently verified.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no observed first-party mechanism detecting and attenuating concrete interference between separate active operational S1 subagents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel agents can work on the same project files, competing for write access and compute budget, but no conflict-specific coordination return is shown. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide changes to separate S1 assignments in response to measured shared-work interference; absent. - Decision owner: TaskManager enforces semaphore and role restrictions, while parent agent issues tasks and consumes their responses; no conflict-specific sibling coordinator identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fixed task cap, parent session/role envelopes, no-delegation subagent mode, task queues and cancellation. - Closure path: generic completed task result or cancellation returns; no shared-write conflict detect→rescope/serialize→later child action response established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the presence of an explorer/planner/coder roster and bounded parallel task manager is generic delegation/capacity control, not organizational S2. - Evidence: src/ankaloop/task.py, src/ankaloop/multiagent.py, src/ankaloop/toolexecution.py. - Basis: bounded negative source inspection. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: external project worktrees/locks could be added by user, but not credited as first-party S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct superior whole-current organizational regulator over all operating child S1 units at the selected user-task recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failed tool calls, per-session step budget, child queue congestion, cancellation, context limits and provider retries are local operational concerns. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent system-wide current resource/priority and commitment allocation with feedback to S1; not found. - Decision owner: main coding model delegates user tasks as needed, task manager schedules by fixed rules, human triggers session/cancellation commands; no separate whole-system current plan/control owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool loop limit, server session manager, TaskManager, event bus and tool/role authorization. - Closure path: local task/cancel/queue state changes, not externally governed whole-current S3 decisions based on system-wide evidence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parent coding actor&#39;s task decomposition and deterministic capacity guard do not prove distinct S3 regulation. - Evidence: src/ankaloop/task.py, src/ankaloop/agent.py, src/ankaloop/server/sessionmanager.py. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: user can govern multiple deployments externally, but that parent organization was not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independently assigned coding-claim audit with independent evidence and binding findings/corrective return path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect code changes and false completion claims; task-local tools and model self-check may encounter them. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a distinct reviewer should inspect producer claims and return failing evidence to a controller with power to block/correct; not shown. - Decision owner: parent/subagent coder/planner and optional memory-review model only review task context or memory facts, not a first-party coding-claim audit mandate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent result, tool observations, user-confirmed hooks, memory review and project rules. - Closure path: normal tool/child result can help a coder, while memory review writes facts; no complementary code-evidence→independent verdict→accountable repair relation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model review of which user facts to remember is not independent verification of operations. Generic planner/explorer subagents do not have a mandatory correctness gate. - Evidence: src/ankaloop/memoryreview.py, src/ankaloop/task.py, src/ankaloop/agent.py. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: user-authored custom reviewer agent could add a workflow not shown as first-party default.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct prospectively selected organizational capability adaptation from external/future environment evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: context loss, stale user preference and repetitive history are addressed by memory, not by separately judging future capability changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a novel or changed skill/system capability based on cross-session environmental opportunities and return a durable operational adaptation; not evidenced. - Decision owner: the first-party dream/memory-review model consolidates facts and user-scoped identity; the installed capability repertoire remains configured or user/plugin authored. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MemoryDreamer, long-term memory store, searchable history, skill loader and session snapshots. - Closure path: project/user facts or global identity reload as context; no autonomous future-capability experiment, option judgment and admission path to alter actual skill/tool repertoire. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fact learning/compaction and identity S5 publication are separate functions, neither by itself supplies prospective S4 strategic intelligence. - Evidence: src/ankaloop/memorydream.py, src/ankaloop/memory.py, src/ankaloop/skills.py, src/ankaloop/memoryreview.py. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: an external adopter may extend skills or capabilities later; no source-backed standard-issue S4 control is imported.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:5\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A(P) - Function: owner-authorized publication and continuation of agent&#39;s durable global persona/identity and role policy, with model-driven extraction and human direct edit/authority modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: persistent identity drift across CLI/server/Telegram sessions, inconsistent user-defined role/style and project-specific persona conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide how to record a legitimate explicit user-defined identity/soul statement, publish to user-scope SOUL.md/IDENTITY.md and return it as precedence-bearing system-prompt context for later sessions. User can explicitly change/overwrite its meaning. - Decision owner: first-party model-directed normal memory tool and separately invokable memory-review actor synthesize durable identity from user-supplied authority (autonomous A path); legitimate user/operator directly defines or edits persona and may invoke commands (parent P mode). No invented self-authorization over third-party organizational policies. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MEMORYGUIDANCE, memory-only review prompt, writesoul/writeidentity/identify tool action, global-only scope check, file persistence, singular persona composition and session-frozen system prompt construction. - Closure path: explicit owner identity/role correction→model-selected memory identity action (normal chat or checkpoint review)→durable global identity/soul file written→new session builds global persona→prepended to base system prompt→subsequent coding sessions operate with updated durable identity. Parent may edit files to override. - Boundary reachability: the memory tool is shipped by default, and MEMORYGUIDANCE is included in every system prompt; on-demand memory review and user direct commands are both first-party operating modes, not developer CI. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model chooses and writes lasting identity text in an installed supported user-authorized path, and the output is made normative in subsequent sessions. User retains ultimate authority; hence A(P), not unconditional autonomous constitutional sovereignty. - Evidence: src/ankaloop/memoryreview.py, src/ankaloop/tools.py, src/ankaloop/memory.py, src/ankaloop/contextbuilder.py, src/ankaloop/commands.py. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: a single global persisted agent identity/persona/values/work-style layer, not project-specific ordinary facts. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: model may select and synthesize durable identity only from explicit user definitions in Base (A) mode; the legitimate user directly defines, changes or replaces global persona in Parent (P) mode. Global-only scope prohibits competing project personas. - Return-to-operation path: a later session prepends persisted Global Soul and Global Identity content to its system prompt, overriding default persona selection after a custom global identity exists. - Basis: first-party model-mediated user-authoritative durable identity decision/return. - Confidence: moderate-high for code mechanism; no live persona-change test in assessment. - Caveats: the model may not reliably update files when not prompted; session persona is deliberately frozen, so updates become active in a new session, not immediately on the same run. The implementation does not govern an external organization&#39;s constitution or validate the truth of every model-generated identity statement.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4e65098f30877ea054f81429644ed0582d035e19/assessments/ankaloop.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4e65098f30877ea054f81429644ed0582d035e19\">4e65098 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-25T23:28:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/limecloud/lime\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Lime</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-25\">2026-09-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/limecloud/lime/tree/3823e9092d4106c877ae08a1d19d593b647cf27d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3823e9092d4106c877ae08a1d19d593b647cf27d\">3823e90 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform an objective into tool-mediated environmental outcomes through repeated model reasoning/sampling, tool action and observation feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing task requirements, workspace/file/process state, tool results and failures, provider output, permissions, user steering and durable mailbox input. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive action/tool and revise subsequent work from returned tool/environment evidence. - Decision owner: the active Lime model agent in each root or child session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapter, current provider turn loop, tool runtime, Thread/Turn/Item projection, session loop, permissions/approvals and persistence/recovery. - Closure path: objective/context → provider/model chooses tool or output → tool runtime applies action → normalized result is appended to the same transcript → subsequent sampling observes the result and chooses the next action → task/environment state changes. - Boundary reachability: standard first-party runtime path used by ordinary root and spawned child sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic tooling executes and bounds actions, but removing the model removes the substantive next-action judgment while leaving only enforcement machinery. - Evidence: providerturn.rs; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate contention among distinct child S1 operations for bounded concurrent execution capacity within one durable root-agent tree. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple child S1s can concurrently demand execution beyond the root-tree child capacity, creating resource contention and unbounded parallel admission. - Decisive decision or feedback right: admit or reject a child turn against root-scoped capacity and release/reuse that capacity as children terminate. - Decision owner: no autonomous S2 actor owns discretionary coordination; the standard path deterministically enforces the configured root-scoped limit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentExecutionLimiter reservations/claims/guards, child residency handling, durable root/thread identity and AgentControl error return. - Closure path: distinct child sessions request execution → limiter observes shared root-scoped active/pending occupancy → atomic reservation admits up to the bound or returns AgentLimitReached → the failed/successful result returns through the AgentControl tool/runtime → the root/child execution pattern changes; completed work releases capacity for later S1 execution. - Boundary reachability: the S2-specific admission/limiting relation is directly on the shipped AgentControl child-execution path: model-visible spawn/follow-up requests reserve or claim root-scoped child slots in App Server before child turns run, and admission failure returns through the ordinary tool/error path; no downstream composition is required to make the contention regulator operative. - Distinct S1 units: each spawned child receives its own durable thread/session and is woken through the normal agent session runtime, so it is a complete operational loop rather than a one-shot model call. - Inter-S1 disturbance: child sessions belonging to the same root compete for the finite child execution slots. - Attenuating coordination relation: first-party root-scoped atomic reserve/claim/release prevents excess simultaneous child execution. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: admission failure is returned as AgentLimitReached; terminal/released slots permit later child/follow-up work. Root model behavior can therefore change after the coordination result. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation specifically regulates an evidenced interaction disturbance between multiple S1 operations — contention for the same bounded execution capacity — rather than merely carrying messages or ordering arbitrary work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the function-specific path is first-party and complete enough to establish S2, but the decisive capacity rule is deterministic/configured. A developer would need to compose an autonomous coordinator/authority to own that discretion, so publication state is C. - Evidence: agentexecution.rs; agentcontrolgateway.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise the current root-agent organization by observing the active durable agent tree and changing current task allocation, child commitments and interventions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing child progress/state, completed/failed work, queued mailbox activity, need for additional parallel capacity, misdirected/obsolete child work and current decomposition of the root objective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide when/what child to spawn, which existing child receives follow-up/current steering, when to wait for results, and when to interrupt a child. - Decision owner: the root model agent through model-visible AgentControl tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable graph/identity store, mailboxes, child session lifecycle, result/state projections, execution limiter, recovery and tool gateway. - Closure path: root agent lists/observes the open tree and/or receives child result/mailbox state → model judges current decomposition/progress → calls spawn/message/follow-up/wait/interrupt → runtime applies the decision to child sessions → child commitments/current operation change → status/result feedback becomes available to the root again. - Boundary reachability: standard model-visible tools are injected into the ordinary first-party runtime and are backed by the App Server&#39;s durable graph/session implementation. - Whole-system current view: listagents enumerates the current open durable root tree and returns each agent&#39;s status and last task message; waitagent returns current child activity/results and state facts. - Current-control decision scope: root can allocate new child commitments, steer existing work, trigger follow-up turns and interrupt active child turns across its current organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: graph/mailbox/session machinery enforces decisions, but the model chooses whether and how to allocate/steer/intervene from current whole-tree feedback. Removing the root model leaves control primitives without the discretionary S3 judgment. - Evidence: agentcontrol.rs; agentcontrolgateway.rs; wait.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary-audit path is established at the assessed root-tree recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as an independent discrepancy between ordinary current-control reporting and operational reality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: arbitrary child agents can inspect workspace state; UI metadata includes verifier/reviewer profiles; repository development/benchmark surfaces contain verification machinery. These do not by themselves establish S3. - Closure path: not applicable; no reviewed standard runtime path establishes complementary access → materially independent audit judgment → finding returned into root S3/control. - Claim being audited: no function-specific first-party audit claim is wired into standard root-tree operation. - Ordinary reporting path: child status, last task, mailbox results and canonical Thread/Turn/Item events flow through the ordinary AgentControl/runtime substrate. - Complementary access path: generic children can be asked to review and can inherit/fork context, but spawnagent exposes a generic task/message/model constructor rather than a function-specific independent audit path; generic prompt expressiveness does not meet Methodology C. - Independence boundary: no standard audit-specific actor, sensor boundary or independently owned verifier closure was established. - Who acts on findings: a root or user could act on an arbitrary review response, but that is a possible downstream composition rather than a first-party S3 closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no S3 function is established before ownership classification. - Evidence: agentcontrol.rs; teamPresets.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop is established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as future/external change requiring adaptation of the root-agent organization&#39;s capability or structure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web/MCP/tool research, provider/model selection, Skills discovery/install/configuration, reusable procedures and persistent task context can extend operation but do not by themselves establish S4. - Closure path: not applicable; no standard first-party path closes external/prospective sensing → generation of organizational adaptation options → S4 judgment → returned capability/current-control change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model research and Skill/tool use are available to S1 tasks; current task learning or user-driven capability configuration is not automatically organizational adaptation. - Evidence: README.md; lime-rs/crates/app-server/src/runtime/skills.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as an identity/constitutional/ultimate-policy matter. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompts, model/provider configuration, permissions/approval gates, tool policies, Skills/profile metadata and user constraints govern operational behavior but do not establish ultimate-policy authority. - Closure path: not applicable; no reviewed path shows an identity/ultimate-policy issue reaching legitimate ultimate authority and returning as authoritative policy governing later root-tree operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: root and child models act within configured operational constraints; they are not shown owning the legitimacy/identity of those constraints. - Evidence: README.md; agentcontrol.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c27b3b98564bb101fde7bbe14fbc11d4a82d12b0/assessments/lime.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c27b3b98564bb101fde7bbe14fbc11d4a82d12b0\">c27b3b9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-04T02:38:11+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/m0n0x41d/haft\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Haft</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-04\">2026-10-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/m0n0x41d/haft/tree/8a5f0384a10003bda7b2b65edc86f0f1a656a345\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8a5f0384a10003bda7b2b65edc86f0f1a656a345\">8a5f038 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform FPF-aware project-governance reasoning such as framing a current problem, exploring/comparing alternatives, recording warranted project memory, or checking a governed concern through a shipped Haft capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous engineering questions, alternative sets, code/project context, source-pattern applicability, evidence/freshness state and reliance requirements requiring contextual rather than fixed decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-backed Haft role decides which evidence/source distinctions are relevant, which current capability applies, what bounded result follows and what minimum non-binding record should be produced when the skill&#39;s persistence contract is satisfied. - Decision owner: the host model executing Haft&#39;s first-party distributed skill/procedure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generated skills/instruction carriers; MCP tools; artifact graph; project ledger; FPF source/query; typed memory; kernel validators and structured errors. - Closure path: operator/current project concern → first-party Haft skill is invoked → model-backed role reads source/project evidence and performs the skill procedure → Haft tool/kernel validates and, where justified, persists the result → later project reads/reasoning consume that returned artifact/state. - Boundary reachability: stable Claude/Codex integrations install the actual Haft skills and MCP server into supported hosts; the procedure and artifact-return path are shipped by Haft rather than authored downstream. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the host model while retaining the kernel leaves validation/storage but removes the contextual reasoning that selects/applies source and constructs the project result. Removing Haft while retaining the host removes the specific role/procedure and project-governance closure being assessed. - Evidence: README.md; internal/cli/skill/h-reason/SKILL.md; internal/cli/skill/h-frame/SKILL.md; internal/cli/skill/h-compare/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Haft is not credited with semantic coding execution performed by external coding agents/runners; S1 here is the first-party governance/reasoning operation that Haft intentionally supplies.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 coordination function was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple hosts/sessions may share one project ledger, but no standard Haft path was found in which distinct same-recursion S1 units create an interaction conflict/oscillation and an autonomous Haft coordinator chooses a response. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S2. - Decision owner: not established as a first-party S2 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: checked project ledger; authority records; artifact graph; host parity; code/project-memory reads; commissions. - Closure path: deterministic consistency/authority checks can constrain several host sessions, but no inter-S1 coordination judgment loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: shared records and concurrency-safe persistence do not themselves establish S2. - Evidence: README.md; internal/cli/skill/h-reason/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an external engineering organization can coordinate multiple agents through Haft records, but its coordinator is outside this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: maintain a project-wide current governance view and close binding choices/execution-authority changes through the legitimate human principal. - Disturbance / variety regulated: active/superseded/stale decisions, unresolved project problems, drift/evidence debt, specification gates, open commissions and current operations that would otherwise rely on ambiguous or unauthorized commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: bind or supersede a current project choice, grant/narrow/decline a bounded WorkCommission, or resolve a material human gate before the affected operation proceeds. - Decision owner: the human principal/operator in the parent-governed mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: h-status project cockpit; Human Gate Brief contract; host-routed h-decide; DecisionRecords; manual h-commission; authority provenance; project/kernel gates. - Closure path: Haft exposes current project decision/drift/commission/spec state → a skill presents the exact current gate/options when a binding choice is required → human principal makes the authoritative choice → supported host route or manual commission path binds it → DecisionRecord/WorkCommission becomes governing project state read/enforced by later Haft operations. - Boundary reachability: the status/gate/decision/commission paths are shipped host integration and kernel contracts; the parent does not need to build a separate governance database or effect sink. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents may analyze options and recommend, but Haft explicitly rejects model-supplied authority and retains the decisive current-control right at the human principal; therefore the published state is P, not A. - Evidence: README.md; packages/haft-pi/skills/h-status/SKILL.md; internal/cli/skill/h-decide/SKILL.md; internal/cli/skill/h-commission/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: not every DecisionRecord is a whole-system intervention. The positive witness is the project cockpit + explicit material gate/authority path that governs whether affected current operation may proceed. - Whole-system current view: compact project status spans active problems/decisions/notes, evidence freshness/drift, commissions, spec lifecycle, coverage and current authority surfaces. - Current-control decision scope: binding current project choices and bounded execution authority at material gates, including whether affected work may proceed and under what scope/evidence envelope.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a recorded decision/claim against current operational reality and feed the verdict back into its lifecycle. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a previously bound decision may have drifted, evidence may have decayed, predictions may fail, or current tests/metrics/logs/code may contradict the recorded claim. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-backed h-verify role chooses and executes discriminating evidence probes, interprets them against declared observables/thresholds, and records an evidence-backed accepted/partial/failed measurement verdict. - Decision owner: the autonomous host model executing Haft&#39;s first-party h-verify procedure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: exact DecisionRecord recovery; baselines; affected-file drift; Bash/Read/Grep/Glob probes; evidence carriers; congruence/evidence expiry fields; kernel requirement that evidence precede measure. - Closure path: recorded decision/predictions → h-verify recovers exact claim/baseline → role gathers current tests/metrics/log/code evidence → attaches evidence and measurement verdict → lifecycle/drift state changes and subsequent reliance, reopen/supersede/hold decisions consume that audit result. - Boundary reachability: h-verify is shipped in the stable host integration and uses first-party kernel evidence/measure/refresh surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic drift and evidence gates support the audit, but the semantic selection/interpretation of real-world probes and verdict is assigned to the model-backed verification role. - Evidence: internal/cli/skill/h-verify/SKILL.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deterministic stale/drift flags alone would be observability; S3 is credited to the separate evidence-against-reality verification procedure and returned verdict. - Claim being audited: a bound DecisionRecord&#39;s declared claims/predictions and continued validity in the current project. - Ordinary reporting path: DecisionRecord, affected-file baseline, implementation/project state and the original rationale/predictions. - Complementary access path: h-verify performs current tests, metrics, log scans and code probes, attaches external/current evidence and checks drift/freshness against the recorded claim. - Independence boundary: verification uses current reality/evidence rather than accepting the decision&#39;s own rationale or status; kernel enforces evidence-before-verdict and preserves the historical claim. - Who acts on findings: subsequent agent work and the human principal consume accepted/partial/failed, drift and freshness results; material reopen/supersede/waiver choices remain parent-governed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime loop was established that senses external/future change, develops project adaptation options and returns a selected adaptation into current capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: evidence expiry, code drift and project-memory change are mainly internal/current governance signals; FPF upstream refresh is repository/product maintenance rather than the assessed project runtime. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S4. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: evidence decay; drift events; source query; FPF refresh tooling; project-memory reconciliation. - Closure path: not applicable at S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: learning/memory/staleness and source-refresh maintenance do not by themselves constitute external-and-prospective organizational intelligence. - Evidence: README.md; packages/haft-pi/skills/h-status/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a host agent can use Haft to reason about future/external concerns, but generic reasoning capability is not a shipped autonomous S4 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: explicit human authority and policy boundaries are strong, but no dedicated identity/ultimate-policy closure for the project organization was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: binding decisions and WorkCommissions may be highly consequential, yet they remain bounded project choices/authority grants rather than necessarily identity-level disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → governing return loop was established. - Decision owner: not established at S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: human-authority provenance, DecisionRecords, WorkCommissions, spec lifecycle gates and kernel authority checks. - Closure path: not applicable at S5. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: preserving the human principal&#39;s authority does not automatically turn every human decision into S5. - Evidence: internal/cli/skill/h-decide/SKILL.md; internal/cli/skill/h-commission/SKILL.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a particular downstream organization might use Haft DecisionRecords for identity policy; that use is not a standard first-party S5 function by itself.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/13474271177db6d48929343a6e681746e3cc59a9/assessments/haft.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 13474271177db6d48929343a6e681746e3cc59a9\">1347427 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-01T23:54:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Git-on-my-level/codex-autorunner\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">codex-autorunner (CAR)</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-30\">2026-09-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Git-on-my-level/codex-autorunner/tree/065f435d6120f95135425d8cc2df52760872a2d3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 065f435d6120f95135425d8cc2df52760872a2d3\">065f435 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute repository-facing implementation, review, debugging or other agent work inside a CAR-managed repo/worktree cell. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository state, code/test/tool feedback, task-specific implementation choices, runtime failures, changing evidence encountered by the configured coding agent and user replies during paused work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next task-specific coding/tool action within the active managed thread or ticket-flow turn and revise later action from returned repository/tool evidence. - Decision owner: the configured autonomous coding-agent actor (Codex, Hermes, OMP, OpenCode or another supported ACP agent) reached through CAR&#39;s shipped managed-thread/ticket-flow adapter path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CAR ticket selection and prompt assembly; managed-thread lifecycle; agent adapters; contextspace; durable transcripts/run state; Git/worktree setup; ticket limits; pause/retry/recovery; permission/runtime configuration. - Closure path: ticket/thread objective + CAR context → configured autonomous agent chooses repository/tool action → action executes in the bound workspace → result/progress returns through the agent session and CAR state → the same active agent cell continues, completes or dispatches a pause/escalation. - Boundary reachability: hub setup explicitly requires a working supported agent, and shipped managed-thread/ticket-flow commands route work into those agents; no application-written integration is required to obtain the autonomous operational cell. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the configured autonomous agent while retaining CAR&#39;s ticket files, queues, worktrees and state machines leaves control infrastructure but no open-ended repository-facing operational discretion. The agent therefore owns the S1 task decision right even though its internal runtime is an external dependency. - Evidence: README.md; docs/AGENTSETUPGUIDE.md; src/codexautorunner/tickets/runner.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this claim credits the autonomous actor only where CAR concretely invokes it. It does not import the external agent runtime&#39;s internal delegation, planning, policy or audit functions into CAR.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate filesystem/Git interference among independently active coding-agent work cells by assigning implementation work to isolated hub-owned worktrees and maintaining explicit resource/thread bindings. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two independent implementation cells against the same repository can otherwise contend over one checkout, branch, dirty working tree and transient repository state. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently active managed threads or ticket-flow cells bound to separate repository/worktree resources, each executing a configured autonomous coding agent toward its own outcome. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent coding cells that share one mutable checkout can overwrite, observe or block one another&#39;s uncommitted state and branch operations; CAR&#39;s PR-mode path is structurally designed to avoid that shared-workspace collision. - Attenuating coordination relation: PR-mode managed-thread creation provisions a fresh hub-owned worktree from origin/&lt;default-branch&gt; (or configured base) and binds the thread to that managed resource; PMA is instructed to reuse a relevant existing active thread rather than blindly duplicate work. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the managed-resource/thread binding determines the filesystem and branch state seen by the agent cell for all subsequent turns. Existing active-thread state is surfaced to PMA before it chooses reuse versus spawning another isolated cell. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive claim is not based on having queues or multiple agents. It is the concrete anti-interference relation independent implementation cell → isolated worktree/resource binding → later actions constrained to that workspace, plus current active-thread visibility used before another cell is created. - Decisive decision or feedback right: establish and preserve the isolated managed-resource binding for a new PR-mode implementation cell rather than allowing independent cells to share the same mutable checkout. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party CAR worktree/thread lifecycle. PMA/operator selects the work to run, while CAR supplies and enforces the S2-specific isolation primitive. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: hub manifest/resource identity; managed-thread lifecycle records; PR-mode worktree creation; workspace setup; durable thread bindings; architecture rule that isolation is structural. - Closure path: request for an independent PR-mode work cell → CAR creates/binds a fresh managed worktree → agent executes subsequent turns only in that resource → lifecycle/progress remain associated with that binding → later PMA/operator decisions observe the managed-cell state. - Boundary reachability: PR mode is a documented shipped car pma thread spawn ... --pr path and the Web/CLI hub manages the resulting worktree directly; the coordination path is not a user-authored plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the PMA may decide that a new PR work cell is appropriate, but the decisive anti-interference rule and worktree binding are runtime semantics. Removing PMA model judgment does not remove the first-party isolation path, so publication is C, not A. - Evidence: src/codexautorunner/docs/managed-threads.md; src/codexautorunner/core/pmapromptbuilder.py; docs/ARCHITECTUREBOUNDARIES.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the credited S2 witness is workspace/Git interference attenuation. No claim is made that CAR autonomously negotiates semantic disagreements or merge conflicts among independent agents.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: maintain a hub-level view of current operational commitments and intervene over which work cell continues, is reused, is spawned, is restarted/resumed, is retired, or is escalated to the operator. - Disturbance / variety regulated: blocked/paused/dead runs; duplicate or stale managed threads; changing workload across repos/worktrees; current delivery/file inboxes; dirty-worktree ambiguity; execution-form choice; active subscriptions/timers; resource and commitment continuity after failures. - Whole-system current view: PMA receives hubsnapshot and explicit current surfaces for run dispatches, managed threads, inbox/file state, managed resources and automation. The hub itself owns the manifest, PMA state, managed-thread records and orchestration metadata across repositories/worktrees. - Current-control decision scope: PMA is instructed to handle blocked runs first, choose inspect/reply/restart actions, reuse a relevant active thread or spawn a new one, choose managed thread versus ticket flow, create PR-mode cells, compact/retire threads, route files and configure automation/subscriptions for continuing work. These decisions change present commitments rather than merely report them. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in PMA mode, choose the current intervention/delegation action over the hub&#39;s active work population; in direct operator mode, the human chooses equivalent current-control actions through the Hub/Web/CLI surfaces. - Decision owner: base mode — PMA autonomous agent; parent mode — human hub operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: hub snapshot/read models; durable orchestration/thread/ticket state; lifecycle reducer and supervisor; worktree creator; CLI/Web commands; restart policy; commit barriers; notification delivery. - Closure path: current hub state → PMA or operator identifies a current-control need → spawn/send/reuse/resume/restart/retire/pause or related command → CAR mutates durable orchestration/workspace state → subsequent worker execution and later hub snapshots reflect the new commitment state. - Boundary reachability: PMA is a shipped hub-level coordinator with first-party prompt, snapshot and CAR command surfaces; direct Web/CLI operation is the recommended self-hosted management surface. Both modes are reachable without custom application code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in PMA mode, removing the PMA actor while leaving supervisors, queues and UI intact removes the discretionary choice among relevant current interventions; deterministic supervisors still enforce recovery policy but do not choose the broader work priority/delegation response. In direct operator mode the same class of decision is intentionally parent-owned. - Evidence: src/codexautorunner/docs/overview.md; src/codexautorunner/core/pmapromptbuilder.py; docs/AGENTSETUPGUIDE.md; src/codexautorunner/core/flows/supervisor.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deterministic ticket ordering, restart limits, commit barriers and state reducers are enforcement/support, not autonomous S3 ownership. The A claim is specifically the PMA whole-hub discretionary path; the parent mode is specifically the supported direct operator control path.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no boundary-reachable complementary audit path was established that is sufficiently independent of ordinary CAR/PMA/worker reporting and returns an audit judgment into current-control correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as a distinct complementary-audit function. CAR has logs, run history, crash artifacts, tests, Git state and optional review work, but these are ordinary evidence/operations unless separately organized into an independent challenge loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an independent auditor. - Decision owner: not established at S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: run history; durable orchestration records; crash artifacts; Git-state capture; commit barriers; CI/tests; operator review; PMA inspection; user-authored review tickets/managed threads. - Closure path: no standard first-party path was found in which a materially independent auditor obtains complementary access, issues an audit judgment and automatically/operationally returns that judgment to change S3 commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: review can be assigned to agents or humans, but the shipped product does not make a sufficiently independent complementary auditor with corrective return part of the standard organization. - Evidence: docs/carconstitution/20ARCHITECTUREMAP.md; src/codexautorunner/core/flows/supervisor.py; docs/car-ticket-skill.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can compose an independent review workflow from managed threads/tickets; generic composability is not a positive S3 state.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then intelligence loop was established that senses external/future distinctions, develops adaptation options and returns a selected option into present CAR capability/S3. - Disturbance / variety regulated: CAR reacts to current requests, run failures, files, SCM/chat events and scheduled wake-ups, but these are current operational/control events rather than a prospective environmental-intelligence function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for prospective adaptation. - Decision owner: not established at S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PMA memory/context docs; automation/subscriptions/timers; SCM follow-up; agent/provider configuration; templates; contextspace; update/runtime state. - Closure path: no first-party path was found from external/future sensing → adaptation-option generation → adaptation judgment → changed current organizational capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: PMA can reason about a user&#39;s present request and schedule future wake-ups, but scheduling, memory and event reaction do not establish the Profile&#39;s external-and-prospective adaptation loop. - Evidence: src/codexautorunner/core/pmapromptbuilder.py; src/codexautorunner/docs/overview.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an external agent run may itself research future options, but that task-level behavior is not a standard CAR S4 closure unless CAR wires it into a prospective organizational adaptation loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure was established at the CAR hub recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operator configuration, permissions, ticket rules, PMA guidance and codebase architecture constrain operation, but they do not constitute an identity-level decision process in the assessed runtime. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for identity or ultimate policy. - Decision owner: not established at S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: hub/repo configuration; PMA AGENTS.md/active context; ticket frontmatter and acceptance criteria; architecture constitution; permissions/credentials; operator Web/CLI controls; static runtime limits and policy checks. - Closure path: no standard first-party path was found by which an identity/ultimate-policy issue is detected or proposed, reaches a legitimate ultimate authority as such, receives an authoritative identity/policy decision and returns to govern later CAR operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: PMA and the operator have substantial current-control discretion, but that is S3 scope. Editing configuration, instructions, permissions or tickets does not by itself establish S5. - Evidence: docs/ARCHITECTUREBOUNDARIES.md; src/codexautorunner/core/pmapromptbuilder.py; docs/AGENTSETUPGUIDE.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: maintainers and operators obviously retain real-world authority over the OSS project and local deployment, but no function-specific first-party S5 closure is reconstructed from that fact alone.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5834c48a0c1e0788c4fb20f803c506bddab2f1d5/assessments/codex-autorunner.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5834c48a0c1e0788c4fb20f803c506bddab2f1d5\">5834c48 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T21:28:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AndyMik90/Aperant\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Aperant</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AndyMik90/Aperant/tree/20250db069a849ab001ac6ab9e3e9779886ab9e2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 20250db069a849ab001ac6ab9e3e9779886ab9e2\">20250db &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform a bounded engineering task by planning, editing, running available tools and reacting to actual build/test/review feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar codebase, missing dependency details, failed subtasks, build failures and QA findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a model-driven session selects code/tool actions and revised attempts within the selected project and configured permissions. - Decision owner: model-backed planner/coder and QA fixer sessions launched by Aperant&#39;s first-party worker/orchestration path; user specifies the task and model provider remains external. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: prompt/config roles, tool registry, session step bounds, task-state persistence and worktree paths. - Closure path: user task/spec → planner output → coder model/tool loop → observed file/tool/test result → correction, remaining subtasks and final QA return. - Boundary reachability: AgentManager&#39;s task-execution path constructs the build-orchestrator worker and worktree with supported defaults; BuildOrchestrator calls real phase-specific sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: code and tool choices adapt to feedback from the model; deterministic phase transitions and limits alone would not supply that discretion. - Evidence: AgentManager active worktree-to-worker path, BuildOrchestrator lifecycle, coder session invocation, first-party agent worker. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for code path, medium for installation-specific runtime. - Caveats: model credentials and actual external provider behavior were not exercised; shipped 2.x is reviewed, not the unmerged 3.0 rewrite.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: mechanically dampen shared-file interference among independently executing coding task cells by giving them separate branches/worktrees and a bounded later integration path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two live task workers writing incompatible paths in the same user repository, followed by textual/semantic merge conflicts at integration. - Distinct S1 units: independently model-driven task/spec coding workers, each performing its own development assignment for the same project. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent edits and changed-file collisions would overwrite one another if both workers used the project&#39;s shared checkout. - Attenuating coordination relation: the first-party launcher assigns a separate branch/worktree per task, and the integration path detects/contains later conflicting edits. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: each launched worker receives its allocated worktree as effective cwd/projectDir and performs subsequent source/tool actions inside it; merge feedback reports conflicts for post-task resolution. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: distinct viable coding cells contend for the same mutable codebase; filesystem partition directly attenuates that interference rather than merely transferring messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party constructor selects a per-task git worktree/branch by default before starting the worker; merge tools detect conflicts and can request AI text resolution after a user initiates integration. - Decision owner: constructor-owned static worktree policy (C), not an autonomous S2 manager deciding live task ownership or negotiated revised commitments; the model resolver selects a merge text only on the post hoc path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: unique spec branch/worktree mapping, git branch isolation, conflict detector, merge report and optional AI resolver. - Closure path: concurrent task launches → distinct project filesystem views → local independent edits without overwrite → explicit integration/conflict detection → accepted/rejected merge result. - Boundary reachability: worktrees are enabled by default in the actual AgentManager call path, and the merge IPC wires MergeOrchestrator and resolver. The no-worktree option and nonfatal worktree creation failure bypass this guarantee. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the decisive prevention is a preset technical partition, not a model-selected inter-agent arbitration; an AI-generated conflict patch after the task does not establish a live S2 policy owner. - Evidence: actual default task isolation and fallback, worktree manager, merge IPC connection, model text resolver. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the isolated mode is a selectable default, not a hard invariant; a failed worktree can revert to shared-root execution, and no model-mediated prospective conflict negotiation was demonstrated.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: let a human desktop operator see ongoing task/agent status, stop or review current work and decide task continuation across a project workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stuck, misdirected, unsafe or unwanted concurrent task execution and acceptance decisions. - Whole-system current view: desktop Kanban board/task progress, agent terminals and active worker/task lifecycle for the selected project are presented to the operator. - Current-control decision scope: operator selects which whole-workspace tasks are allowed to run, stopped or accepted; the app applies that choice through live worker kill and state updates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the operator chooses which task to start/stop and approves/rejects a review result using IPC connected to active worker lifecycle and task-state manager. - Decision owner: parent/operator (P); App/AgentManager provides a shared current status/command surface but no autonomous whole-portfolio executive deciding priorities and allocations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Kanban task board and multiple agent terminals, process registry, task-state transitions and stop/restart tracking. - Closure path: active task state appears in desktop → operator inspects portfolio/task → stop or review command → worker termination or status/acceptance transition → refreshed UI. - Boundary reachability: README documents the active desktop task/terminal modes; TASKSTOP and TASKREVIEW handlers call the live AgentManager/task-state paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: UI and preset concurrency bounds do not make a model the owner of whole-current resource/priority control; the consequential selection remains human. - Evidence: supported desktop task controls, stop and review IPC, worker lifecycle. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: no independent runtime UI exercise; the right is scoped to the app&#39;s tasks and does not imply a whole business enterprise controller.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the coder&#39;s completion claim against direct code, changed-file and test/visual evidence before considering the task done. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a coder may have skipped acceptance criteria, introduced a regression or reported passing work without sufficient testing. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate model-driven QA reviewer runs inspections/tests and writes a pass/fail report; a fail invokes a different fixer session and a fresh reviewer. - Decision owner: model-backed QA reviewer distinct from the coder, with BuildOrchestrator interpreting the QA result and launching correction; the fixed loop bound is constructor policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: explicit QA reviewer prompt, raw git diff/source reads, test and UI checks, qareport status, reviewer/fixer phase separation and report reset. - Closure path: coder finishes → QA reviewer separately inspects repository/diff and tests → failed report → QA fixer repairs → previous report cleared → new reviewer pass or bounded failure. - Boundary reachability: BuildOrchestrator calls qareviewer and qafixer in the production task lifecycle; prompt demands actual direct inspection rather than relying on coder prose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the independent QA model decides evidentiary defects; a deterministic CI gate, status parser or coder self-check alone would not receive S3 credit. - Claim being audited: implementation satisfies spec acceptance criteria and can be signed off. - Ordinary reporting path: the coder&#39;s subtask status/summary and implementation-plan progress. - Complementary access path: a new QA session with tools to inspect git diffs, source, unit/integration/E2E tests and rendered behavior when relevant. - Independence boundary: roles and session calls are separated; same overall app/provider may be used, so independence is contextual/role-based rather than an external audit organization. - Who acts on findings: the separately launched QA fixer performs repair and returns work for another QA review; operator may later accept the task. - Evidence: QA reviewer/fixer bounded correction loop, reviewer&#39;s direct-evidence protocol, distinct agent configuration. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: source-backed closure path, not proof of an observed provider-backed successful QA run; review quality and UI tool availability remain environment-dependent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party closed loop was demonstrated that revises the harness&#39;s future organizational capabilities in response to external strategic change. - Disturbance / variety regulated: available memory observations, prospective feature ideas and competitor context might support future project decisions but do not establish a harness-level adaptation return. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no source-backed owner of external/future viability adaptation and accepted persistent organizational capability change. - Decision owner: the user chooses roadmaps and future tasks; model sessions can propose project features, while the passive memory observer classifies own-task tool/retry signals. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: memory candidate capture/trust gates, optional co-access synthesis (marked placeholder), roadmap discovery and feature-generation files. - Closure path: roadmap.json or memory candidates can be persisted and later consumed; this is project work/planning and episodic memory, not a demonstrated external change → selected future operating policy/capability → adoption loop of Aperant itself. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: feature priority proposals and internal task reflections are not by themselves an S4 system-level adaptation function, even when generated by an LLM. - External distinction: optional competitor context for a user project&#39;s roadmap is not evidence of continuous environmental intelligence governing the harness. - Future / prospective distinction: the roadmap is prospective for project features, but no first-party acceptance/implementation of a changed harness operating architecture is linked by this path. - Evidence: roadmap generated as project JSON, memory observer candidates and gate, deferred synthesis path, roadmap feature prompt. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a user could adopt generated feature plans outside the runtime, but this is not credited as a shipped S4 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime authority closes the ultimate identity, legitimate organizational purpose and constitutional policy return of a multi-agent system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: security permissions, task approvals and provider config constrain execution but do not constitute identity-level governance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established; project owner and external product governance retain final authority. - Decision owner: user chooses projects, task requests, permissions and acceptance; fixed safeguards apply without an identity-level deliberator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: command validators, OS/path containment, OAuth/provider selection, task approval and status controls. - Closure path: authorization gates restrict tasks; no first-party reasoned ultimate-policy amendment and return into subordinate decision rights. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: local allowlists and prompts are procedural bounds, not the authority to resolve legitimate purpose and identity. - Evidence: security profile and validator, bash validator, operator review. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high within reviewed boundary. - Caveats: upstream product governance is outside the harness runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/47c78a1757f6c5a60e23b7f75c653bac4b285109/assessments/ap/aperant.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 47c78a1757f6c5a60e23b7f75c653bac4b285109\">47c78a1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T23:12:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/RunMaestro/Maestro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Maestro</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/RunMaestro/Maestro/tree/32ecbc14fe832138e9a7af4f263d1026a8aa8a5b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 32ecbc14fe832138e9a7af4f263d1026a8aa8a5b\">32ecbc1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute project/coding tasks inside individual provider-agent sessions controlled through Maestro. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended codebase state, implementation uncertainty, tool output, tests and task-specific context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/research/tool actions needed to complete the assigned prompt/task. - Decision owner: the model-driven supported coding agent running as a Maestro session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session spawning/resume, project cwd, provider CLI integration, Auto Run task feeding, worktrees and message transport. - Closure path: Maestro sends a task/prompt to an agent session; the agent performs work and returns output/state; Maestro records and can feed the next task/turn. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: provider models own operational action choices; Maestro supplies the first-party harness that invokes and preserves their sessions. - Evidence: README, Group Chat docs and provider/session architecture. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: coordinate multiple agent sessions so work is routed to suitable agents while avoiding concurrent edit interference and stale shared-project state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two agent processes can edit the same files simultaneously, overwrite each other and continue from stale state; multi-agent questions can also require selective delegation/follow-up across project contexts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which agents to involve and whether more rounds are needed; standard availability policy holds a request when the selected agent is already busy. - Decision owner: the model-driven Group Chat moderator owns agent/round selection; deterministic Maestro availability machinery enforces the selected coordination safely. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: “Only work with agents that are free” default, busy-state detection/holding, per-agent process/workdir, @mention routing, isolated worktrees and group message queue. - Closure path: moderator selects agents; Maestro delays any busy conflicting target until free while other agents proceed; returned responses go back to the moderator, which follows up or synthesizes before returning to the user. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime&#39;s busy check is enforcement, not the organizational owner; the autonomous moderator decides participant selection and continuation, with the safety relation altering when chosen S1 work actually runs. - Evidence: docs/group-chat.md, README and Group Chat source modules. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: worktrees/queues alone would not establish S2; the positive mapping relies on the documented same-file concurrency disturbance plus moderator/availability feedback loop. - Distinct S1 units: separate local or remote provider-agent processes, each in its own agent/project context. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent processes editing the same files can overwrite one another and leave both conversations acting on stale state. - Attenuating coordination relation: the moderator routes work while the default availability policy holds requests to busy agents; separate worktrees can additionally isolate intentionally parallel writes. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: busy/free state delays or releases the requested invocation, and member responses return to the moderator for follow-up/delegation/synthesis. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the cited first-party behavior exists specifically to attenuate a concrete interaction hazard among distinct active agent processes, not merely to pass messages.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-system current-control function is established beyond task/session orchestration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider errors, cost/usage, session state and playbook progress are monitored, but no separate whole-fleet current-control owner with organizational discretion is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party owner was found that sees the whole system and reallocates current resources/commitments/priorities on behalf of the whole. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: usage dashboard, Auto Run tracking, resilience/failover, queues, session discovery and remote control. - Closure path: no qualifying distinct S3 loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: moderator routing is counted as S2; dashboards, retries and failover are observation/runtime recovery rather than a demonstrated agent-owned S3 decision right. - Evidence: README, Group Chat docs and architecture. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit loop is established by the standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: code diffs, agent output filtering and moderator follow-up were inspected, but they do not constitute independent organizational audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct reviewer/auditor right with complementary evidence access and corrective return was found. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: diff viewer, logs/history, moderator synthesis/follow-up and provider-native tests/tools. - Closure path: no first-party independent audit finding → correction/reassignment loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a moderator asking for more information or a human inspecting diffs is part of normal operation/review, not an evidenced independent S3 channel. - Evidence: README, Group Chat docs and architecture. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate external/prospective adaptation loop is established for the Maestro fleet. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing provider availability and future project needs were inspected, but existing resilience is operational recovery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party owner was found that senses external/future distinctions, develops adaptation options and returns a selected option into present capability/S3. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider resilience/failover, documentation graph, usage analytics and user-authored playbooks. - Closure path: failover/retry restores current operation; it does not close an outside-and-then capability adaptation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: recovery from overload/quota and analytics are S1/support mechanisms unless connected to prospective option generation and capability change. - Evidence: README features and architecture/docs. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established for the Maestro fleet. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user specifications, playbooks and app settings constrain tasks but do not establish organizational identity authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party ultimate-policy decision path with legitimate authority and return was found. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user prompts/specs, playbooks, moderator settings, agent/provider configuration and read-only mode. - Closure path: configuration and prompts affect tasks directly; no identity-level authority closure is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a user being able to configure agents or choose a moderator is not sufficient for VSM S5. - Evidence: README, Group Chat management docs and application configuration surfaces. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9f83e41179680c24cbe23a5091abda11fcc221d0/assessments/maestro.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9f83e41179680c24cbe23a5091abda11fcc221d0\">9f83e41 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T16:18:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/termide/termide\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TermIDE</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/termide/termide/tree/351a551ad234ea5071a8a4fe226e172dcb5d76ee\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 351a551ad234ea5071a8a4fe226e172dcb5d76ee\">351a551 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously inspect, modify and verify a software project through model-selected coding and tool actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar repository state, implementation choices, tool/test failures, provider variation, permission refusal, context pressure and user steering. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/tool actions, interpret evidence and choose the next engineering step. - Decision owner: the active built-in model-backed TermIDE Agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, provider adapters, permissions, plan guard, queues, compaction, sessions/checkpoints, MCP/web transport and cancellation. - Closure path: user/current goal → model selects action → runtime executes and returns evidence → model revises work or completes the turn. - Boundary reachability: ordinary built-in TermIDE agent panels and built-in headless execution instantiate this loop without requiring a custom external agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic machinery constrains and transports action; the model owns the open-ended engineering choice. - Evidence: agent.rs; agent tools; README. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external ACP agents are separate systems and are not used for this claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 coordination function is established in the frozen standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no concrete concurrent peer-S1 interference is placed under a first-party attenuation loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generic task delegation, sequential tool execution, permissions and message queues route or constrain work but do not coordinate distinct simultaneously active first-party S1 units. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task delegation requires user-supplied custom agents, and the frozen design says parallel delegation is not implemented. - Evidence: task.rs; agent design. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic extensibility is not credited as an already closed S2 function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the active autonomous goal commitment by deciding, from a whole-session current view, whether work is complete or must continue. - Disturbance / variety regulated: premature completion, incomplete evidence, remaining failed/missing work and mismatch between the user-stated goal and current operational evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue DONE to release the current goal commitment or CONTINUE with the most important missing item, causing another work turn. - Decision owner: the separate model-backed goal judge invoked by first-party /goal mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: transcript cloning, bundled goal prompt, verdict parser, iteration cap/scheduling and panel event routing. - Closure path: operational turn finishes → judge receives the full current transcript plus goal → judge returns DONE or CONTINUE → DONE ends the goal; CONTINUE becomes the next work prompt → subsequent current operation changes. - Boundary reachability: /goal is a shipped built-in command and uses the built-in Agent.judge path plus bundled goal prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code schedules and parses the verdict; the model judge makes the substantive completion/continuation judgment. - Whole-system current view: the judge receives a copy of the complete current built-in-agent transcript for the active goal, including prior user turns, assistant messages and tool results. - Current-control decision scope: whether the current whole-session goal commitment terminates or remains active for another operational turn, including the missing item returned to that turn. - Evidence: agent.rs; goal prompt; submit.rs; agent docs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic user pause/stop is not used for this state; the claim rests on the autonomous /goal completion/continuation loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material complementary audit function with independent access to operational reality is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the goal judge checks completion but receives the ordinary session transcript rather than a materially different evidence path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /goal judgment, plan mode, checkpoints and ordinary verification evidence can improve confidence but do not create complementary independence. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the goal judge is a separate model call but audits only the normal transcript; richer reviewer agents require user-supplied custom definitions. - Evidence: agent.rs; goal prompt; task.rs. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an extension can define a reviewer, but an uninstantiated extension point is not a standard first-party audit loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no external/future distinction is transformed into adaptation options and returned into current organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web tools, MCP, session history, compaction, handoff briefs, skills and model/tool configuration provide information or continuity but do not close S4. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: web research within a current task and forward-looking handoff text are operational/context mechanisms, not a durable prospective adaptation conversation with S3. - Evidence: handoff.rs; web tools; toolset.rs. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: future custom agents/skills could implement adaptation, but that is outside this frozen standard-distribution closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level or ultimate-policy issue is routed to a legitimate authoritative owner and returned as governing runtime policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission modes/rules, system prompts, toolset switches, plan mode and user configuration constrain operation but do not create S5 closure. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: coding and goal-judge agents operate inside developer/user-authored rules and cannot redefine TermIDE&#39;s identity or ultimate policy. - Evidence: permissions.rs; toolset.rs; plan prompt. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: operator permissions/configuration are ordinary operating constraints rather than identity governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ce6a9548f30532663494b756f29dd8a1a7a8fef3/assessments/termide.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ce6a9548f30532663494b756f29dd8a1a7a8fef3\">ce6a954 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T22:10:34+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ace-agent/ace\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ACE</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ace-agent/ace/tree/82709de050e1db6e6ef2f07bcb0393560b94992a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 82709de050e1db6e6ef2f07bcb0393560b94992a\">82709de &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce domain-task answers and revise the working solution with input, playbook and environment/target feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous questions, contexts, mismatches against targets, reasoning errors and model/tool failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Generator makes answer/reasoning choices; Reflector diagnoses error and proposes corrective reflection; Curator chooses playbook operations that change subsequent generation. - Decision owner: first-party configured model-backed Generator, Reflector and Curator acting as a linked operational learning cell, with deterministic correctness check and parsing as enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: answeriscorrect, updatebulletcounts, applycuratoroperations, JSON parsing, retries, token limits and configured step frequencies. - Closure path: sample → generate → check/reflect → revise candidate answer and playbook → post-curator answer, then changed playbook reaches later samples/windows. - Boundary reachability: offlinetrain, onlinetrainandtest and batched mode call these steps in the shipped run paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model selects response content and playbook changes; the Python shell does not supply these discretionary answers/operations. - Evidence: ace/ace.py (trainsinglesample, offlinetrain); ace/core/curator.py (curate); ace/core/reflector.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for configured source behavior, not measured model effectiveness. - Caveats: evalonly intentionally does not run the adaptation path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference when independent sample/reflection and curator-proposal cells attempt to contribute overlapping or conflicting modifications to one shared playbook. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel curator cells can propose duplicated/conflicting updates and cause shared-playbook order dependence. - Distinct S1 units: multiple parallel generator/reflection sample cells and multiple parallel curator proposal groups over separate sample subsets within ACEBatch.trainbatch. - Inter-S1 disturbance: proposals from simultaneous groups may duplicate insight bullets or produce incompatible add operations; applying each immediately would make resulting knowledge order-dependent. - Attenuating coordination relation: all groups read the same frozen baseplaybook, only propose operations, and a single model-backed Curator.aggregateoperations reducer merges/deduplicates before atomic applycuratoroperations. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the reconciled shared playbook is passed to parallel post-curator generation and later samples; contradictory independent writes are not directly committed. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: unlike generic fan-out/fan-in, the reducer resolves conflicting/redundant knowledge contributions into one coherent shared operating resource. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which candidate ADD operations survive and how to combine them; Python enforces single-application order. - Decision owner: model-backed Curator reducer, with deterministic snapshot/aggregation and schema checks as support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: thread pool, group sorting, common snapshot, operations schema, reducer prompt, once-only playbook update. - Closure path: parallel proposals → reducer selection → updated playbook → revised answer generation in the same batch. - Boundary reachability: source-distributed ACEBatch.trainbatch calls proposeoperations, aggregateoperations and applycuratoroperations; depends on configured batched path and nonempty proposals. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reducer model resolves semantic overlap; deterministic code performs serialization only. - Evidence: ace/acebatch.py (trainbatch three phases); ace/core/curator.py (proposeoperations, aggregateoperations). - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium, conditional on nontrivial batched groups and model outputs. - Caveats: batching and generic task sequencing alone do not count; the positive evidence is the specific shared-playbook conflict reducer.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: regulate present training-system performance and decide which installed playbook to use for final operational evaluation. - Whole-system current view: periodic aggregate training and held-out validation accuracy summarizes current performance across sample cells, not merely one answer. - Current-control decision scope: save the best aggregate-performing playbook and select that snapshot for final test under a global configured criterion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: local learning steps can improve one sample while degrading aggregate validation accuracy or consuming the configured training budget. - Decisive decision or feedback right: periodic aggregate validation computes accuracy and saves the best-scoring self.bestplaybook; final test uses that selected snapshot rather than automatically using the latest playbook. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party ACE orchestrator, applying the user-configured validation criterion and schedule; no model-owned whole-run intervention is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: evaluatetestset, evalsteps, bestaccuracy, self.bestplaybook, saved checkpoints. - Closure path: sample-level operational updates → aggregate validation feedback → selected best playbook → final test generator. - Boundary reachability: ACE.offlinetrain updates bestplaybook, and ACE.run(mode=&#39;offline&#39;) explicitly calls runtest(... playbook=self.bestplaybook) when test samples exist. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the criterion is fixed, selection is a numeric comparison, and model agents do not negotiate cross-cell performance commitments or revise global budgets. - Evidence: ace/ace.py (offlinetrain periodic validation, run final test). - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: offline mode requires supplied validation/test data and user configuration.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no separate complementary access to actual operations beyond the normal answer-check/reflection and validation workflow. - Disturbance / variety regulated: wrong generated answers are noticed, but the same ordinary training process uses target data and prompts the Reflector; no sporadic independent probe of actual operations is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: answeriscorrect and evaluatetestset score the known sample targets; Reflector.reflect receives the same trace/prediction plus the supplied target, and directly participates in the production correction loop. - Decision owner: regular orchestrator and model-backed Reflector, not an independent metasystem audit owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task processor, answer comparison, logs, reflector prompts, validation metrics. - Closure path: correction loops back to the same generator; this is ordinary learning/QA, without a complementary inspection channel to S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model critic is agentic, but its mandatory in-line function and information sources do not establish independent audit of ordinary operational claims. - Evidence: ace/ace.py (trainsinglesample, runtest); ace/core/reflector.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: independently annotated targets still enter regular training and testing, not a separate surprise-audit access path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no established externally and prospectively oriented intelligence function despite durable playbook learning. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the incoming task distribution and correctness feedback alter task-solving heuristics, but neither future external trends nor prospective strategic options are modeled. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Curator chooses new reusable bullets based on recent reflections, current context and token budget, not observed shifts in the outside environment or future risks/opportunities. - Decision owner: model-backed Curator for in-domain learning; no first-party S4 decision owner at this organizational boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: playbook text, curator operations, offline/online training windows, validation and saved best playbook. - Closure path: learned bullets do return to present execution, but the required outside-and-then sensing/option-development half of the S3–S4 loop is absent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic experience replay, memory consolidation, reflection and improvement of later answers alone are insufficient for S4; the Curator has agentic operational learning rights only. - Evidence: ace/ace.py (trainsinglesample, onlinetrainandtest); ace/core/curator.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for the path distinction, without live evaluation. - Caveats: future task distribution could shift, but no prospective external-trend/options loop was located.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate runtime ultimate-policy/identity deliberation path at the assessed ACE recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model provider choice, token budgets and evaluation/training settings are launch-time constants; corrective bullet changes do not arbitrate mission or values. - Decisive decision or feedback right: external experiment operator specifies the task, config, models and ground-truth availability; code does not escalate ultimate-policy conflicts to a parent and return ratified decisions. - Decision owner: operator/developer outside the first-party runtime&#39;s accountable policy path; model agents choose task answers and playbook tactics, not legitimate identity governance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configs, static prompts, token budgets, mode validation, error paths. - Closure path: no policy-level issue-and-ratification return is found, only ordinary config and supervised training. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a prompt and configurable constraint are not S5 authority. - Evidence: ace/ace.py (init, extractconfigparams, run); ace/prompts/curator.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: human maintainer governance outside the runtime does not establish a first-party policy return loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9688f00cf70ec16d346559f291e1f66f00e836e5/assessments/ac/ace.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9688f00cf70ec16d346559f291e1f66f00e836e5\">9688f00 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T19:01:48+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bastani-inc/atomic\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Atomic</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bastani-inc/atomic/tree/d6bc44cf753bd2748ffac6dd994205a170216741\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d6bc44cf753bd2748ffac6dd994205a170216741\">d6bc44c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: adaptively execute coding tasks with tool use and observation-conditioned model choices. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown repo state, test failures, tool errors, partial files, requirements ambiguity and changed task context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select coding action, inspect/change files, run commands and change the next task step after observing tool results. - Decision owner: packaged Atomic model-directed coding actor; generic pi-agent-core provides an SDK loop as a dependency of the published package, while Atomic session and bundled tool/extension execution implement an agentic coding harness. No external vendor coding CLI is silently credited. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Atomic AgentSession, configured agent-core, first-party tools, session prompt/continue, persistence, provider adapters and optional subagents/workflows. - Closure path: user task→AgentSession.prompt→model response through dependency→first-party tool results→agent continuation and revision/next action→completion. - Boundary reachability: installed CLI exposes coding task paths in default modes, distinct from optional multi-stage workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model selects contextual next operations; guardrails, session state and extension hooks constrain rather than predetermine coding behavior. - Evidence: packages/coding-agent/src/core/agent-session-prompt.ts, packages/coding-agent/src/core/agent-session.ts, packages/coding-agent/src/core/tools/edit.ts, packages/coding-agent/package.json. - Basis: packaged first-party coding actor and dependency-labeled model/tool loop. - Confidence: high that the shipped path exists; no live model performance claim. - Caveats: the dependency supplies loop mechanics; no novel Atomic implementation of external SDK internals is claimed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent concrete cross-worker shared-checkout interference in a configured parallel coding-task group. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two independent model-driven coding workers writing inconsistent changes to the same repo checkout or colliding when publishing a named output. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose isolated sibling Git worktrees with checked task CWDs; reject duplicate output paths and avoid concurrent write interference in the shared mutable checkout. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic subagent orchestration constructor with optional worktree flag; not an autonomous coordinator model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: createParallelWorktreeSetup, Git worktree per task, independent child CWD, collision-safe output publication and explicit failure when worktree setup incompatible. - Closure path: parallel work request with worktree option→first-party preparation isolates each coding S1&#39;s writable tree and validates CWD/output paths→child workers operate in separated worktrees→per-child results and patch artifacts return to parent without racing on the same working files. - Boundary reachability: packages/subagents is bundled in shipped Atomic package; parallel executor actively calls setup in worktree-enabled supported mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: concrete interference is attenuated by deterministic isolation, so constructor C, not A. Mere parallel spawn or messaging without isolation would not qualify. - Evidence: packages/subagents/src/runs/foreground/subagent-executor-parallel.ts, packages/subagents/src/runs/foreground/subagent-executor-worktree.ts, packages/subagents/src/runs/shared/worktree.ts, packages/subagents/src/shared/exclusive-file-publication.ts. - Distinct S1 units: separate model-directed coding-agent children in a parallel execution group. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping writes to the shared Git working directory or duplicate output publication paths. - Attenuating coordination relation: per-child Git worktree isolation, validated CWDs and duplicate output publication blocking. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: accepted child execution paths move to isolated worktrees and conflicting publication is refused before the worker result can be treated as a valid shared artifact. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: construction specifically changes the shared mutable write domain, attenuating interference in the same resource two S1s would otherwise contend for. - Basis: source-proven explicit mode-specific coordination construction. - Confidence: high for static supported worktree path. - Caveats: off by default when worktree false; isolation does not prove autonomous merge resolution or elimination of semantic conflicts between patches.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: parent-controlled current management of a whole durable coding workflow and its live stage commitments, failure/resumption and approvals. - Disturbance / variety regulated: blocked/failed stages, developer interruptions, changing priorities and budget/time or review gate status within an ongoing multi-stage run. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect an overall run/stage state then pause, resume/steer or quit a run, supply human gate decisions and return worker execution to an authorized state. - Decision owner: human operator through first-party workflow command/TUI; model may orchestrate stage-level tasks but an autonomous distinct whole-current S3 decision owner was not demonstrated. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable store, stage/run snapshots, workflow status and run-control commands, pauseRun, resumeRun, quitRun, human-input gate and stage admission/resume logic. - Closure path: run-wide status/stages/blocker observations→operator command/approval choice→persisted control transition→stage work is paused, resumed or aborted with observable new state. - Boundary reachability: bundled workflow extension registers shipped /workflow and tool controls and status inspections for user-run workflows. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parent P covers actual whole-run operational intervention rather than mere UI or local tool permission; no S3=A is claimed. - Evidence: packages/workflows/src/extension/workflow-run-control-command.ts, packages/workflows/src/extension/workflow-tool-control.ts, packages/workflows/src/extension/workflow-human-input.ts, packages/workflows/src/durable/resume-runtime.ts. - Whole-system current view: run stage status, progress and resumable/paused state, including reviewer outcome when configured. - Current-control decision scope: pause/resume/quit active workflow stages and human advancement through gated work. - Basis: parent-governed current whole-workflow path. - Confidence: medium; specifically workflow mode. - Caveats: workflow stage sequencing or default single-agent approvals alone would not support a distinct whole-current S3.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: separately model-driven reviewer tasks verify a distinct producer/orchestrator&#39;s engineering claims using code/evidence and feed binding findings to the workflow&#39;s next action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate implementation looks complete but violates original objective, is incorrect, lacks validation or has regression/security flaws. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reviewer A and B each issue independent judgment on code/evidence; both must give parsed acceptable verdicts for approval, otherwise continued correction or explicit unapproved handoff. - Decision owner: separate first-party ralph reviewer model tasks (within packaged workflow), distinct from the producing orchestrator. The convergence arithmetic is a deterministic guardian of their agent judgments, not the author of correctness claims. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ctx.parallel reviewer calls with distinct role/model configs and evidence reads; parsed review decision contract, reject-invalid/convergence logic, consolidated findings, bounded independent reverification and next orchestrator iteration informed by reports. - Closure path: implementation/research evidence from orchestrator→two independent reviewer task sessions inspect repo delta and evidence→structured findings + typed approve/incorrect verdict→ralphReviewConvergence checks→two-approval gate or consolidated findings/reverification→next model-directed implementation/repair, or explicit nonconverged outcome at bound. - Boundary reachability: installed ralph builtin declared in workflow builtin manifest, wired by runRalphWorkflow; no external CI or human-authored review prompt is needed when this supported mode is invoked. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: substantive pass/fail evidence and advice belong to separate reviewers, not the same producer and not a fixed program-only checker. - Evidence: packages/workflows/builtin/ralph.ts, packages/workflows/builtin/ralph-runner.ts, packages/workflows/builtin/ralph-review-gate.ts, packages/workflows/builtin/review-convergence.ts. - Claim being audited: correctness, scope fidelity and sufficient proof of orchestrator-produced code changes. - Ordinary reporting path: orchestrator task report and implementation notes. - Complementary access path: new reviewer tasks read research, implementation notes and orchestrator report, and prompts require source-delta checking against base branch. - Independence boundary: distinct reviewer task invocations with reviewer A/B model configurations, separate from producer; both must independently pass, though provider-level independence and real-world results are untested. - Who acts on findings: workflow loop consolidates/reverifies and returns negative findings into bounded next orchestrator implementation; unapproved completion stays distinguishable from success. - Basis: source-confirmed packaged, mode-specific auditor-decision-return closure. - Confidence: medium-high for first-party wiring, no live witnessed audit. - Caveats: does not imply default coding chat or every workflow automatically runs two reviewers. This is operational reviewer independence of sessions, not separately attested external review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separately owned external/future intelligence cycle that selects and returns a prospective change to Atomic&#39;s own sustained capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: iteration feedback, reviewer findings, memory pressure and task-specific research are about completing an ongoing coding objective, not reconfiguring future system capacity based on external futures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select a prospective capability/strategy change across future tasks in response to outside/environment intelligence; not identified. - Decision owner: workflow orchestrator and reviewer model focus on present task, human may update configuration or packaged workflow outside run but no functional S4 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: research stage, resumable checkpoints, reviewer/repair iterations, skill and workflow authoring; none alone qualifies as an installed outside-and-then decision-owner. - Closure path: reviewer feedback returns into present implementation attempt; no independent external change forecast→future-capability design→adoption into stable operational harness found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the ralph research stage and retrospective repairs serve current work; mere extensibility and maintainer releases cannot donate organizational S4. - Evidence: packages/workflows/builtin/ralph-runner.ts, packages/workflows/src/authoring.ts, packages/coding-agent/src/core/agent-session-auto-compaction.ts. - Basis: bounded negative source inspection. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: external adopter could author a self-improvement process, which is not established by this frozen supported boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct ultimate organizational identity/policy adjudication owner at the installed coding-work recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsafe tools, acceptance policy and authorization boundaries are operational enforcement, not demonstrated identity/purpose conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: make rightful top-level purpose/identity interpretation and return binding policy authority to operational/current and future systems; no function owner evidenced. - Decision owner: user can approve gates and configure workflow/agent resources, while code enforces fixed bounds; no installed S5 organ with legitimacy/purpose remit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: approval gates, role/tool constraints, task authorization, repository instructions and user settings. - Closure path: configured command/human gate can permit or stop a workflow stage; this is S3 parent/current policy, not an ultimate-identity S5 decision loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: having a human confirmation or immutable acceptance criteria does not automatically make the coding harness an organization with final identity/policy owner. - Evidence: packages/workflows/src/extension/workflow-human-input.ts, packages/coding-agent/src/core/agent-session.ts, packages/workflows/builtin/ralph.ts. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: adopting organization may be S5 parent but is outside the first-party installed harness boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8a2c38ea094030bbe0be712185fb9d86be49977d/assessments/atomic.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8a2c38ea094030bbe0be712185fb9d86be49977d\">8a2c38e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T22:42:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/manishiitg/agentworks\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentWorks</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/manishiitg/agentworks/tree/5d53bfbb1541567fe3cb40b6a1a54197f2676fdc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5d53bfbb1541567fe3cb40b6a1a54197f2676fdc\">5d53bfb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce workflow/crew task outcomes and correct bounded operational failures through shipped model-driven action/tool turns. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous incoming tasks, tool execution results, provider changes, partial runs, errors and varying workflow context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-driven workflow/crew actor selects substantive task actions and, in Pulse&#39;s allowed repair scope, chooses corrective steps from run evidence. - Decision owner: the first-party instantiated workflow/Pulse agent at the task-cell boundary; user specifies goals and external models/tools execute supplied requests. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: schedule/trigger transport, workspace permissions, session/run identity, crew dispatch, tool APIs, durable SQLite, retry/timeout and receipt validators. - Closure path: configured workflow input → runtime agent/tool turn or crew dispatch → artifacts and terminal run evidence → subsequent agent/Pulse decision or returned result to the requesting workflow. - Boundary reachability: the supported scheduler invokes the agent session through startSessionInternal and the crew step runner dispatches first-party configured crew triggers with a polled terminal result; neither is only a development example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the discretionary task content and repair selection are model-agent choices; removing that actor leaves transport/enforcement but no equivalent autonomous semantic task resolution. - Evidence: README; scheduler.go; crewsteprunner.go; workflow.go. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: shipped first-party composition, not an observed native model/provider E2E success. External coding CLIs retain their own internal agent loops and are not treated as AgentWorks-owned mechanisms.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference between separately dispatched crew cells/retries that otherwise reuse one workflow run&#39;s delivery/result boundary. - Distinct S1 units: separately configured crew/worker cells and repeated attempted deliveries of a workflow step operating against the shared run/task state. - Inter-S1 disturbance: duplicate dispatch of an already live/successful step, cross-group result adoption when a workflow folder is reused, and conflicting outcomes from replayed crew triggers. - Attenuating coordination relation: the first-party runner uses immutable execution identity plus group/step/trigger/destination in a delivery key, adopts existing live/successful deliveries, explicitly retries only failed terminal outcomes, and keeps group delivery results isolated. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the crew step polls an existing/accepted terminal outcome and returns it to the invoking workflow step; the caller then advances or errors rather than launching a competing duplicate actor. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it is an explicit collision/retry interference contract for parallel/repeated crew operations sharing one execution identity, not merely a mailbox or DAG. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicated or misattributed crew work and inconsistent shared run completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: resolve which crew execution is authoritative for a specific task/group and feed that resolution back into subsequent step execution. - Decision owner: a first-party deterministic workflow-specific coordination primitive; task/crew configuration and broader autonomous multi-unit regulation still require composition by the workflow developer/operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delivery keys, user-scoped trigger permissions, terminal polling and execution ID persistence. - Closure path: attempted crew delivery → conflict/adoption check → existing or new run terminal status → result/error returned to the workflow step. - Boundary reachability: RunCrewStep is a server-owned runner bound on ordinary UI, Builder, scheduled, webhook and workshop workflow paths; dedicated tests cover groups, duplicates, retries and run-folder reuse. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: this narrowly evidenced S2-specific contract is not an autonomous coordination judgment over all conflicting S1 units; the deterministic key and composer-selected groups justify a constructor C, not A. - Evidence: crewsteprunner.go; crewsteprunnertest.go; sub-agent delegation guide. - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: ? - Function: proposed cross-workflow current-status, goal/cost and operational-exception supervision; the evidence does not establish a complete decisive whole-installation control/return owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent workflow health failures, cross-workflow cost/goal exceptions and resource/commitment conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: prioritize current installation-wide interventions or reallocate commitments using a whole-system view. - Decision owner: operator and/or Chief-of-Staff proposal agent are described, but the exact shipped decision path versus dashboard/reporting support remains insufficiently verified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: run and cost ledgers, Org cards, Pulse verdicts, notification channels, configured budget/permission gates. - Closure path: per-workflow evidence is surfaced through dashboard and proposals; an authoritative cross-workflow decision returned to reallocate live work is not unambiguously closed by the inspected first-party shipped path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a health dashboard, scheduled reporting and bounded per-workflow fixers do not themselves own discretionary whole-system current control. The README/design describes an Org Pulse path, while source includes notices that an additional Chief-of-Staff product branch is unmerged. - Evidence: self-improvement and reporting; scheduler.go; server.go. - Basis: structural - Confidence: low - Caveats: a concrete supported parent-governed S3 current-control feedback loop remains the unresolved shipped path, not a hypothetical feature request. Do not infer S3 from a scheduler or cost enforcement alone.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: challenge workflow self-reports and remediation claims through an evidence-specific review-and-verification path before declaring an outcome fixed. - Claim being audited: a workflow ran correctly, achieved its declared goal, and a proposed fix passed a real subsequent run/evaluation. - Ordinary reporting path: the workflow writes run outcome, artifacts, summaries and status; the scheduler retains its run evidence. - Complementary access path: Pulse&#39;s typed review finding, verification and worklist paths inspect run/eval/report/KB evidence and record failed, inconclusive or passed checks in SQLite rather than accepting final-answer prose. - Independence boundary: perspective-specific reviewers and optional delegated specialists can inspect evidence separately, but current Pulse resumes the same workflow conversation and permits one owning agent to consolidate; mandatory materially independent challenge is not established for all normal runs. - Who acts on findings: the Pulse Review+Fix actor may apply bounded repairs, pending user decisions handle material changes; scheduler validates terminal receipts and preserves missing-review recovery state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect self-reported success, unverified repair claims, stale artifacts and missing evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue an evidence-backed contrary review/verification result and require the workflow/fixer to follow it before closure. - Decision owner: packaged review and verification agent/tool path; composing an independent specialist and accountable corrective return is supported, but not inherently closed for all default workflows. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trusted session registry, SQLite lifecycle tables, typed findings/verification tools, terminal receipt validator and scheduler turn ordering. - Closure path: run evidence → due review/findings → bounded Fixer or pending approval → later producing run/evaluation verification → recorded closed/reopened/inconclusive finding and later workflow/Pulse behavior. - Boundary reachability: runPulseLifecycle, typed worklist/result guards and module execution are shipped in the normal server; optional specialist compositions use product-supported delegation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a distinct audit-specific evidence path exists; default same-session reviewer identity and deterministic receipt enforcement are insufficient for A independence, so the supported constructor is credited narrowly as C. - Evidence: pulseconsolidation.md; scheduler.go; pulseworklist.go; pulsereviewtoolstest.go. - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: reassess future workflow effectiveness against goals/changed evidence and adapt the operating strategy, while retaining a separate parent approval mode for consequential changes. - External distinction: goal progress, evolving task evidence, model cost/tier trends, report/evaluation quality and updated external knowledge/requirements compared with earlier runs. - Future / prospective distinction: a working implementation can still be off-goal; stale assumptions or an ineffective strategy will harm later workflow capability even when current runs complete. - Adaptation option generated: strategyauditor/goaladvisor recommend changed approach; technical/knowledge reviewers propose refreshed reports, learning and tools; bounded repairs can be selected and applied by the agent. - Path back into current capability / S3: accepted safe fixes alter workflow artifacts/knowledge/steps and subsequent producing runs verify impact; material strategy/goal proposals enter an approve/reject/defer user decision and later approved workflow configuration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing goals/evidence, strategy ineffectiveness, knowledge decay and mismatch between current workflow behavior and future desired outcome. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select bounded safe prospective workflow improvements autonomously; separately approve or reject consequential strategy/goal changes under legitimate user authority. - Decision owner: model-driven Pulse Review+Fix agent for bounded autonomous improvements; installation user/operator for material strategy or goal changes in the parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Gate due-worklist, typed pending decisions, impact ledger, freshness stamps, backup/publish and verified change receipts. - Closure path: cross-run/external evidence → reviewer/Goal Advisor options → bounded agent fix or user approval → changed workflow/knowledge/plan → later run/eval/impact feedback. - Boundary reachability: the normal post-run scheduler resumes the first-party workflow session for due reviewing/fixing, and shipped pending-decision tools/Workflow UI expose approval return for material changes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in the base mode the model-driven actor chooses adaptive fixes, not the scheduler&#39;s fixed cadence. In the distinct parent mode, the user retains the consequential adaptation choice and its result is returned into workflow operation; these are alternative rights, not simultaneous owners.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party unresolved ultimate-identity or whole-organization policy adjudication/return loop established at this installation boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: goal configuration, workspace permissions, provider constraints, run approvals and operational user preferences were checked as plausible but non-qualifying paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying runtime identity/ultimate-policy right exposed beyond ordinary parent authoring or local policy enforcement. - Decision owner: operators configure goals and permissions; Go enforces settings and agents perform tasks, but no organizational S5 decision process is evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fixed auth/workspace/folder policies, approval tools, goal scorecards and user config surfaces. - Closure path: ordinary config/approval changes constrain later execution; this is not a reconstructable unresolved identity matter → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative identity-policy decision → returned operation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prompt identity, role/goal labels, safety policy and approval workflows are not evidence of decisive S5 ownership. - Evidence: README; human feedback system; server.go. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/95070c9689bc1b58a9db0c4574a38c2fb297c59a/assessments/ag/agentworks.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 95070c9689bc1b58a9db0c4574a38c2fb297c59a\">95070c9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
